   103	`ed.sh overlap`, first run: 12 unprotected runs of 8–17 words. Seven were re-composed: six ordinary phrases ("in the middle of the afternoon and it"; "thing he had seen any of them do"; "and it was a strange thing to be"; "she looked at the five of them once"; "and turned them over and looked at the"; "he stopped a stride and a half off") and the slip's wording, which had followed the packet's own summary of it ("seventy marks on the map paid at Oxhollow"; the slip now reads *Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.*). Final run: the five packet lines above.
   104	
   105	## New canon minted (flag where marked)
   106	
   107	- **The Oxhollow slip (flag):** real paper, a careful unpractised hand, the hold's mark a horned head, broad between the horns, over three wavy strokes. *The high traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. To be walked with a cart, and mapped. Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.* No coin is left in Pike's box; the hirer pays on delivery and marks the slip.
   108	- **Why the job exists (flag):** an Oxhollow cart fetching Thornwater grain goes round by Lowmarch's ferry, eight days each way; the salt crews speak of a shelf over the high ground between, and a mule string once came over it in three days. Oxhollow is "a ford with a fence round it"; its keeper takes a copper a wheel.
   109	- **Which way it is walked (flag):** east to west. The eastern foot is at the lower of the two crossings on the Thornwater road, where a side water comes in from the north; an old cart-track, grown over, climbs it for two hours to a gorge. Thornwater is not visited and the stretch is not walked.
   110	- **The smith's terms (flag):** the cart is the party's own, sold on day 12 and cut to a mule's gauge (four feet between the wheels, five from hub to hub). Six marks laid down against its return, which is the hire; six more if it does not come back. Paid at first light on the 71st: in hand, forty-six. It is drawn by hand, two in the shafts.
   111	- **The boxes go (flag):** the ferryman is filling the loft over the drum with hay against the cold and wants all of it.
   112	- **Brom's strap:** a doubled bight of the ferryman's long rope over his head and one shoulder, made fast low on the cart's near side; he draws with his chest and keeps both hands behind him. On the bench he walks on the up-slope side and leans against it. **The ferryman's rope went into the gorge with the cart.**
   113	- **Shale-backs, as seen (flag):** as long as a cart and broader; four legs like tree-stumps; back and flanks roofed with overlapping horn plates, keeled, the largest along the spine, lichen growing on the old ones; a small head carried at knee height, ending in a beak like a tortoise's, with which they scrape lichen and moss off rock; they shed plates (grey, the size of a shield, built in leaves like a hoof). Half-grown calves have paler plates and keep to the inside. They mow a shelf in a line, a length and a half apart, and the line moves about forty yards an hour without any animal being seen to walk. Sounds: rasping beaks, and plates clattering "as a slate roof settles".
   114	- **Pike's two tales (flag):** a salt string on the cold road, under a ledge herd, whose Force shifted a fallen block with his Path, and "the hill came down, with the herd upon it"; and roofing-stone cutters who used to come up the side water with carts and one year did not come back. Pike could not say which kind of loud.
   115	- **Karis's derived page (flag), as read out:** *Shale-back. Seventy-fifth day. One: it does not answer fire at three hundred and fifty yards, nor sound, nor a blow on its own shelf. Two: it answers something given off by one of its own in fright, at once, the whole herd together, by moving away. Three: by that, and by the carters' rule, it will answer a declaration in the same way, and a declaration is a great deal larger than a frightened animal. Derived. Not tested.* Copied into Cael's log in his words, with what he saw at the west end. On the map's margin it reads as derived on the seventy-fifth day and used on the seventy-eighth, and Karis will not write that it is tested, since she could not see both ends of her line.
   116	- **The mountain (flag):** the fan (two hundred and sixty yards, a gully along its gorge-ward edge, rock islands; climbed in six stages); the bench (thirty feet at best, pitched outward a hand or two to the yard; slab, with tongues of loose plates where gullies cross); a stone dropped from the first hundred is heard at a count of four and a piece, and again off the far wall; four sleeping places under rock, each found by Seln; eight narrowings (eleven feet; nine; nine and a half; seven; eight; six and a hand; five feet nine with a crust at the rim; the eighth nine feet wide and broken clean across by a slot of two feet); the bay (two hundred yards by forty, floored with shale, a seep at its eastern corner); the pale rock, or fin (see the event list's amendment); the ledge (three hundred yards, six to eight feet wide, roofed in parts); the lip (thirty-one paces, four feet wide, tilted, forty-four scratched stones); the shoulder; a mule track down the western side; a valley road north-west to Oxhollow, a day and a half.
   117	- **The map (flag):** one long pasted leaf: two ruled lines for the shelf, with crosses for hollow places, hatched tongues for loose stone, a black bar at the eighth, and nothing drawn beyond the bay; beside it six columns (number, width, pitch, footing, water, and whether it takes a cart, with a reason); a separate column numbering the narrowings; the cart's own gauge in a box at the head. **Sixty-three lines**, sixty-two in Cael's hand and the last in Karis's. The verdict is Karis's last column and Pike's later line: carts go round.
   118	- **The stampede, as fixed:** see chapter 19 and the event list. Lira's burst is involuntary and is her own Path. Karis's line is nine points and eight channels, about sixty yards, held eight seconds; it empties her. Three or four heavy falls are heard after the cart; nobody sees them. From the western ledge on the 81st the herd counts twenty-five.
   119	- **Karis's lamp:** a small brass reading lantern that lives in the left-hand box with her pens.
   120	- **Oryn, as seen (flag):** not tall, square in the shoulder, broad in the hand, hair cropped as short as a boy's; a man's brown coat made for a bigger person and taken in ("off a peg that nobody was using any more", given by a hold in her first year); a low hoarse voice; a small brown mule with a pack-saddle, two panniers and a crossbow in an oiled cover lashed on top. She stops a stride and a half from people. Cael puts her at about ten years older than himself; no age is stated. Her right heel is rubbed raw and taped, and has been for two rounds.
   121	- **Tide, as taught (flag):** contact only. What mends is the patient's own current, which stands at a break "like water behind a board"; she finds where it stands, opens the way with her own, and holds it open while the body does six weeks' knitting in under an hour, and she pays for the six weeks. A reserve "like a purse", spent at the rate of what it mends: three mendings in a day if she has slept under a roof and been fed, two on a road. She will not touch a table until she has eaten and slept two hours. She cannot do it to herself. She mends what she can read. **The surface reading:** hands on the hurt, or two fingers on a wrist and two on the neck, for a count of ten; it costs her nothing she would notice. **The deep reading ("the long one"):** both hands, all of her, minutes, the patient still and whole, at a mending's cost; not run on a road. A person's current runs in a bed, and "the shape of the bed is their Path"; she names Karis as Ember by it. **The stall:** a hand on a wrist for a breath as the other means to declare, and the declaration does not come; it costs a day's mendings and the next day's and mends nobody; she has done it and hates it. No count of times is given.
   122	- **Tape (flag):** linen strip three fingers wide, spread with pitch, mutton fat and something from a hold that keeps bees. Five bands from breastbone to spine, laid on the out-breath. Twenty-one days from the 78th; she means to lift it herself. For Lira's ankle: an anchor turn above the joint, two strips under the heel and up the outside, and a figure of eight.
   123	- **Brom's wrist:** mended on the 80th; a cuff of plain linen "to remind the hand"; a week from the middle of that day: it takes what sits on it and nothing that hangs from it.
   124	- **Oryn's route, as told (flag):** seven holds, twenty days, "thirty-odd" rounds; late eleven times and this the twelfth, five days behind; nobody has put a coin in her hand past the Line; holds feed her and the mule, one shoes the mule, one gave the coat. She began with four holds and added the fifth in her third round, the sixth "for an old woman's sake" and the seventh because of a child. In a city she was one of six on a healing house's staff, where the morning list is kept by tier. No city is named.
   125	- **The carter's hand:** the carter who told her of the late crew, at the foot of the lip, had a split palm that she had promised to do that evening; he hauls to Thornwater.
   126	- **Oxhollow (flag):** on the north bank of a broad shallow river, in a hollow, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate on the south side toward the ford and another on the north; a ford of a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of water; a yoke of red oxen with a chain, for drawing carts through; the keeper's low stone house with a turf roof between the gate and the water; a long house; a well; the healer's table of scrubbed ash on two trestles under a lean-to against the gate-house, with a bench along the wall. The keeper is old, bent, with a notched hazel tally-stick, and cannot read; he keeps his coin under a hearthstone. He would have sent three carts over the top at the next dry spell with a Force walking behind.
   127	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the smith; the ferryman's boy; Oxhollow's keeper; the woman who keeps Oxhollow's gate; the woman with the scald, the ox-driver, the boy with the collarbone, and five others on the bench; the man with the shoulder, the nursing mother and the old herdsman at the next hold; the child and its mother at the hold after; the carter with the split hand.
   128	- **Teague (flag):** two holds' work in four weeks, a drain cut and a byre roofed; two mules. The bow's limp is an old hip that went out and seated itself wrong, ten years or more ago. Teague timed his second slip to meet the healer's round at the upper hold on the 81st and waited a day. On the hill above Oxhollow he asks her whether the hip is a thing she would do ("Not on a road. Not in a night."), and she tells him his five are in the yard ("They're not mine." / "No. They're not anybody's. I noticed that.").
   129	
   130	## Owner flags (for a ruling)
   131	
   132	1. **Length.** 42,012 against 40,000. Chapter 21 (6,088) and chapter 22 (6,347) carry the over-run: the lip and the walk down took their planned room, and day 84 holds four Cael scenes and two windows. Say if it should come down, and where.
   133	2. **Window lengths.** Oryn's window is 2,098 words against 2,600 planned, and Teague's 1,264 against 1,400; the movement's secondary share is 12.5% against a planned 15%. Both windows do what the packet lists. Either can take another scene if the book-level share needs it.
   134	3. **Progression vocabulary** is about 55 per 10k on a strict list against a planned ≈78. Tide is taught in full and the herd's sense is derived on the page, but four chapters are mountain work in which Cael spends nothing. Say if the count should be raised by more log entries or by more named refusals.
   135	4. **How the flight is derived.** Nobody declares under the herd. Karis's evidence is: a flint fire in plain sight (nothing); a rock striking the herd's own shelf (two animals step); and one frightened animal on a falling plate, at which all thirty step uphill together, the far end before the sound could reach it and round a rib of the hill. She concludes that the herd has "the same sense, or its cousin" as the hounds, for whatever a frightened shale-back gives off, and that by the carters' rule it will answer a declaration likewise. **She does not say that the animals have Paths or give off discharge**, and Brom's objection ("They've no Paths") is left standing. The bible calls these animals Path-deformed; the party does not know that phrase.
   136	5. **Derived, not tested, after the stampede.** The herd runs from Lira's burst and parts round Karis's line, which a reader will take as a test. On the page Karis keeps her word "derived": the map's margin says "derived on the seventy-fifth day, and used on the seventy-eighth", and she declines "tested" because she could not see both ends. Lira's thought in the window is that Karis has "staked the whole page on it".
   137	6. **The fin lies across the slope**, not down it (event-list amendment). It stops stone and would not have stopped the animals. "At eleven paces" is the apex of the line above the wall's middle.
   138	7. **Why one more loud thing turns it.** Cael's reasoning is that a declaration in front of animals already running from one would dam the eastern stream, and a dammed stream goes over its bank, which is Karis's line. That is the whole of the tactical argument, and it is mine.
   139	8. **Karis lays points at a distance** of up to about thirty yards, from standing, in some ten seconds, and joins nine of them. Edition Book 3 gives her single points at nine paces and channels of up to eight paces; nothing I found states her farthest point. It empties her, which is the cost. Say if the range needs a limit.
   140	9. **Lira's burst throws her about twenty feet**, back onto sound ledge, and the crack is what crosses the gorge. I read the packet's "her own Wind, full, crosses the gorge" as the sound and the discharge, not as her body.
   141	10. **The stall** is named once (ch22:43), in Oryn's mouth, with no count of how often she has done it. BOOK_MAP C8 allows at most one mention and notes that the owner may prefer none; it comes out with five sentences.
   142	11. **Oryn's words for the two readings.** She calls them "the short one" and "the long one", and says once that a healing house calls them the surface reading and the deep reading. The protected lines say "the long one"; the bible says surface and deep.
   143	12. **"A river in the dark" is spoken to Karis**, with Cael on the table and the other three in the yard. Oryn explains "banks" when Karis asks: the bed a person's current runs in, whose shape is the Path. She says only that in Cael "it's moving, and it hasn't a side anywhere that I can find." I took out a clause that had her say there was "more of it than there ought to be", so that Movement 5's "More than one" keeps its place.
   144	13. **A plant for the deep reading.** When Oryn says "both hands, and all of me… minutes", the narration notes that four people in the yard know what else those words describe, and that Karis has gone white. Nothing is said aloud and Oryn does not see it. Cut it if Movement 5 should come to that thought first.
   145	14. **What the five have cost Teague** (a day waited at a hold for the healer, for the bow's hip) is mine. He learns it from Oryn on the hill and from Oxhollow's yard and keeper, and nothing about the five that a yard would not show. The short exchange between Oryn and Teague is in her window only.
   146	15. **Seln goes down for help** at first light on the 80th and returns ahead of the healer's mule. She had started up before he met her; the protected log line ("because a carter said we were late") stands.
   147	16. **Twenty-five animals** are counted on the 81st. I did not enter five as dead; the text says so.
   148	17. **The ferryman's rope is lost**, and so are the blankets, the kettle, the canvas and the flour. Nobody has yet said what is owed for the rope.
   149	18. **Lira's right boot** went under the plate; she is barefoot on that side, taped, through day 84, as Movement 4's entry requires. Cael's coat has now lost both sleeves (the right one bound her splints).
   150	19. **Brom's hedging bill** survived on his belt and was the lever under the plate. The ash wedge is not mentioned in these chapters; if it was on the cart, it is gone.
   151	20. **"Nineteen" does not appear** as a word anywhere in the eight chapters (a count in chapter 16 stops at twenty-two; the pace on the lip is the twentieth), following Movement 2's practice.
   152	21. **No name was needed.** No new person, place or animal is named. The mule is "the mule". Oryn's city is unnamed.
   153	
   154	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   155	
   156	**Calendar.** Dusk on the **eighty-fourth day past the Line**, on Oxhollow's bank. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term" twice; "frost"; "the next dry spell"). Days on the page: 70, 71, 72, 73, 74, 75, 76, 77, 78, 79, 80, 81, 82, 83 (in passing), 84; and, in Oryn's window only, 86, 89, 91 and 92. Weather: dry roads; first ice on a kettle on the 74th and frost on the bench each morning after; a cold rain on the 79th; milder in the valley. The next movement opens on Oxhollow's bank at dusk on the 84th, or the morning after.
   157	
   158	**Where everyone is.** The five are at Oxhollow, in the yard and at the ford, with three boxes on the ground against the gate-house wall. Oryn has gone north out of the gate at dusk with her mule, and is due at Thornwater on the 93rd. Teague and his three have crossed the ford southward at dusk with two mules, a day ahead of the five on the Lowmarch road.
   159	
   160	**Cael.** Eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted anomaly.** **Deployment since the Line: one** (the ford burst, day 53). Nothing was used on the mountain or since: Anchor, Compression, the Iron read, Wind and Storm all offered and were refused; Shadow sealed. **Three ribs broken, left side, low, on the 78th; taped with five bands on the 81st; twenty-one days from the break, and Oryn means to lift the tape herself.** Left calf opened along the old bite on the 78th, bound in Oryn's linen, not mended. No log entries for the 78th and 79th. He has been read three times by a healer (the 80th, and twice on the 84th) and has answered three questions truly: his hurts knit in the right places; faster than other people's; no healer has ever got anything from him. He has not asked Oryn south. He has not answered Hesk's letter. Teague has spoken to him and he has said one word.
   161	
   162	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. **One involuntary full burst on the 78th**, when the ledge went; none refused more hardly than the second. **Right tibia broken on the 78th, set and mended on the 80th: straight, new, to walk within the week and not to be run on for a month.** **Right ankle never mended** ("That's a tape and a season. It'll set."); she tapes it herself as Oryn taught her and says that she now knows where it is. Her right boot is under the mountain; she is barefoot on that side. She rode Oryn's mule from the shoulder to Oxhollow and has not said the word. She asked for the ankle once.
   163	
   164	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No declaration; the Skin did not start under the cart at the eighth narrowing. **Left forearm opened at the wrist on the 78th (two boxes), mended on the 80th; in a linen cuff; a week from the middle of the 80th, to take what sits on it and nothing that hangs from it.** He carried the third box down the mountain on his back. He has the hedging bill.
   165	
   166	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. **One declaration on the 78th: nine points and eight channels, held eight seconds. Empty since;** told to ask nothing of herself for a week from the 80th. She could not strike a flint on the 78th, wrote one line on the 82nd and held the purse on the 84th. Holds: the long leaf's fair copy is with Oxhollow's keeper (see below); the derived page (shale-backs), in her hand, with the ford finding beside it in Lira's; everything in the three boxes; the carter's map. She has met a primary source who keeps no book, and has asked her nothing she was not invited to.
   167	
   168	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. No Path lifted. Unmarked. He found every sleeping place, noted the pale rock, crossed the live slide for water, walked the lip twice and marked forty-four stones, and went down for help on the 80th. He lent Karis his coat from the 78th. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.** He gave reports unasked on the 72nd, the 75th and the 79th, since each bore.
   169	
   170	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, said by herself. She knows that she cannot read Cael, by surface reading, hurt or whole, and that his ribs knit "with nothing doing it"; she knows nothing of fragments and has no theory. She has promised him the long reading "when you're not broken", and has put one day on her route for it (her window, the 92nd). She is five days behind her round. She has mended six times on the page: Lira's leg and Brom's wrist (80th); a scald, a foot and a collarbone (84th); and, in her window, a shoulder at two mendings' cost (86th). She keeps no record.
   171	
   172	**Teague.** At Oxhollow's ford at dusk on the 84th he said his four things and crossed. He knows: no cart; a leg and a wrist mended by the route's healer; the girl's foot strapped; the small one asleep sitting up; the boy's ribs taped; that the mountain came down and all five came off it; that the keeper paid seventy for a no. He does not know what Cael is. He and his three (the spear, the bow, the boy) and two mules are a day ahead on the road south.
   173	
   174	**Objects and money.** **The cart is in the gorge**, with the ferryman's rope, the blankets, the kettle, the canvas and the flour. Three boxes, whole; the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. Seln's case. Hesk's satchel, with the log, the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and Pike's lines on bark. Karis's lantern, cord, brass straightedge and Hesk's clock. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two marks earned** (62 + Oxhollow 70); **a hundred and sixteen in hand**; **six owed to the smith** for the cart. Pike's slip is in hand, with five strokes and Oxhollow's mark at its foot, to be carried to the plank. The long leaf was unrolled on the keeper's board and paid for there; the text does not say who carries it away, and the keeper is its buyer.
   175	
   176	**Pike's book and the wall.** Unchanged since the 69th: six lines, twelfth of thirteen, Teague eleven up. The traverse is not yet said.
   177	
   178	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: the five, and nobody else. Oryn knows that a healer's hands cannot read him and that he heals fast; she has been told nothing. Oxhollow's keeper knows that a Path used under the shale-backs brings the hill down, as derived. **Nobody has mentioned quiet ground.** Oryn's other holds are unnamed; Thornwater and Oxhollow are the only two named.
   179	
   180	**Open threads.** A fourth reading, at Thornwater, in nine days, and what four of the five felt when a healer said "both hands, and all of me". Twenty-one days of tape. Karis's reserve. A leg that may not run for a month and an ankle that will set. Six marks owed for a cart, and a rope. What Pike will write for a job that delivered a no and lost a cart. "The same contract inside a season." Five animals fewer on a hill, heard and not sworn. Two hounds still open below Thornwater's ford. Lira's question, now with a job in it and a ledge under it. Hesk's letter, and no roof.
