    30	## The ground, as I fix it
    31	
    32	- **Which way they walk it.** The slip names the traverse from the hirer's end (Oxhollow to Thornwater). They are at Lowmarch, a day and a half from its eastern foot and six days from Oxhollow by road, so they walk it east to west and come down on the hirer's side with the map in hand.
    33	- **The eastern foot.** At the lower of the two crossings on the Thornwater road (the first one met from Lowmarch; the ford of Movement 2 is the upper), a side water comes in from the north. An old track goes up it for two hours to where the valley shuts into a **gorge**. They do not pass Thornwater, and the stretch is not walked.
    34	- **The fan.** At the gorge's mouth, on its south side, a fan of shale scree comes down from the end of a shelf: two hundred and sixty yards of slope, at the pitch of a barn roof, with a gully along its gorge-ward edge.
    35	- **The bench.** A shelf along the gorge's south wall, high above the water, running west: thirty feet wide at best and six at worst, **pitched outward like a roof**, floored with slab, with aprons of scree where gullies cross it. The face of the mountain rises on the left hand; the gorge is on the right. The gorge **gives every sound back late**, off its north wall.
    36	- **The unit.** A *hundred* is a hundred of Cael's strides, at a yard to the stride. Lira runs each hundred out and back before the cart is asked to go on it. At the end of each hundred Karis takes the **width** across the shelf with her knotted cord (in feet; the cord's whole length is never stated), the **pitch** (hands of fall to the yard, by a pebble on a thread hung from the brass straightedge) and the **depth** (a stone dropped and counted). Cael writes one line to the hundred: number, width, pitch, footing, water, and whether it takes a cart. Seventy marks on some sixty-odd lines: he is paid by the line.
    37	- **The cart** is their own old cart, bought by the smith on day 12 and cut to a mule's gauge, hired back. It is drawn by hand: two in the shafts (Seln and Cael), Brom on a breast-strap on the up-slope side.
    38	- **The herd's shelf** runs along the face three to four hundred yards above the bench. The herd drifts west along it at about the party's pace.
    39	- **The narrowings**, numbered as they come: eleven feet, nine, nine and a half, seven, eight, six and a hand, five feet nine, and the eighth, which is wide enough and **broken clean across**: a slot two feet wide with edges like a sawn board.
    40	- **The bay.** Beyond the eighth the bench opens for two hundred yards into a bay forty yards deep, floored with a scree apron. Seln's overhang and a seep of water are at its eastern end.
    41	- **The fin.** In the bay's western third, a blade of pale hard rock stands out of the scree and runs straight down the fall line: fifteen yards long, head-high at its upper end (the nose), twice that in the middle, a yard or two thick. On its down-slope side there is a hollow of bare floor, where slides have parted before and not filled in.
    42	- **The ledge.** From the bay's western end to the mountain's shoulder: three hundred yards of level shelf, six to eight feet wide. Its last part hangs under the shoulder. Beyond the shoulder the track goes down the western slope.
    43	- **The lip.** What is left where the ledge went: a scar four or five feet wide, tilted outward, loose with plates, thirty-one paces long, above the gorge.
    44	
    45	## Chapter 15 — day 70 to the foot of the fan (day 72)
    46	
    47	1. **The plank, the seventieth morning.** Present: the five, Pike, the meadow. The bodies as the packet enters them (the calf a leg on the flat and nine uphill; Brom's wrist closed, wrapped, not to pull; Lira's ankle bound and stiff; Karis's palms two pink lines each). Fifty-two in hand. The slip is on the tack, on real paper, with a horned head over three wavy strokes: the high traverse, to be walked with a cart and mapped; seventy marks, on the map, paid at Oxhollow. Pike reads it twice. Lira, who had had enough of fords, reads it and finds a mountain with the ford only at the paying end.
    48	2. **He counts why.** Fourteen slips read as always; this one sorts to the top, and he makes himself say why, in three items: it pays (more than all six of their lines together); nobody's finger is on it (no HOLD, no captain's name; Teague is out on two holds' slips); it is *ground*. Four half-mended people can measure. What changes: he sees that he has chosen a job for having no opponent in it, and enters that as a want.
    49	3. **Pike: take a cart.** Cael asks whether it can be walked and measured without one. Pike's packet line, whole. Pike says what he has heard of the bench (salt crews; a mule string, once) and that shale-backs keep that mountain. Hands; five strokes.
    50	4. **Seln at the plank's end.** While the others go for the smith, Seln stays and asks Pike something, low. Cael sees it from the meadow and does not go to listen: a refusal made on purpose, the one he promised at this plank.
    51	5. **The smith.** Their own cart, shorter in the axle. Six against its return, and six more if it does not come home. Brom walks round it as round a horse he sold. **The boxes:** the ferryman is filling the loft over the drum with hay against the cold; there is nowhere to leave them; they go on the cart, with the reserve sealed in the third.
    52	6. **Day 71.** The coin paid at first light. The saddle; the wash; the cart drawn by hand; Brom not allowed in the shafts and inventing the strap. Camp above the wash; a flint fire.
    53	7. **Day 72.** The lower crossing; north up the side water; the track; the gorge heard before it is seen, giving the cart's noise back late. The fan. End on the fan seen whole, and a grey plate in the scree that is not stone.
    54	
    55	## Chapter 16 — the fan and the first of the bench (days 72–74)
    56	
    57	1. **Seln's report (it bears).** The plate is horn. Seln says what he asked at the plank: the carters' rule, in Pike's words (**protected: "Never be below them. Never be *loud* below them."**), and two tales that end with a mountain coming down, neither of which says which kind of loud. Cael's ruling: the hold stands, for everything any of them carries, and now every kind of quiet with it.
    58	2. **The first pull.** The loaded cart will not climb. Brom: a cart on scree "floats"; do not roll her, lift her head and let her swim. Unload. The boxes go up by hand (Seln and Cael one between them, in nines; Brom one on his back in a strap: carrying is not pulling). Cost: the calf shuts three times on the slope.
    59	3. **The haul (day 73).** The ferryman's rope over a rock horn at the fan's top; the cart on the upper end; the haulers walk *down* as she goes up, Brom in a chest loop as the counterweight, hands behind him. She floats sideways toward the gully on the second pitch; Lira, sitting braced behind a boulder with a tag line round her back, holds her head up. Karis at the horn calls the rope. Four pitches. Nothing is used: Anchor asks about the horn, Compression about the strap, and the Iron read offers him the whole weight of the slope, which is the one thing a surveyor would most like to know.
    60	4. **The bench.** The first hundreds: Lira runs, Karis's cord, the pebble on the thread, the stone dropped and the count that comes back twice. He writes the first line and is told by Lira what he is being paid for it. Growth: the habit he was mocked for is a trade.
    61	5. **Seln's overhang.** At dusk Seln is already there, with a floor swept and water found. Day 74 opens: hundreds nine to twelve; and above them, on a shelf of the face, a line of grey plated backs that he first takes for the mountain.
    62	
    63	## Chapter 17 — the herd and Karis's day (days 74–75)
    64	
    65	1. **Thirty.** What a shale-back looks like and sounds like (plates like roofing slabs, lichen on them; a low beaked head; a noise like a slate roof settling). He counts thirty, and looks for the one that moves first, as he found it at the ford, and there is none to find. They are below them, since the bench goes nowhere else.
    66	2. **A cheap test.** That evening Karis asks for a fire: a flint fire in sight of the herd, which can be put out. No head comes up. Voices next: the gorge has been giving Brom's tail-board back all day. Two of the country's three are struck off. The third cannot be afforded.
    67	3. **Karis's day (the learning encounter).** She asks for a day and gets it. A seat on a slab; Hesk's clock; Cael as her second pair of eyes and her hand when hers tires. (a) **Spacing:** a length and a half between each along one contour. (b) **The line moves** forty yards in an hour and she has not seen one animal take a step. (c) **A rock** comes down from above and goes between two of them, bounding: each takes one step aside, and no other head lifts (a step, not flight). (d) **The plate:** a slab lets go under the hindquarters of the easternmost; and all thirty, along three hundred yards, some of them round a shoulder of the face, step uphill together. No first animal. She saw them step, and counted, before the sound reached her.
    68	4. **The page.** Laid beside the ford page (Lira's copy). The hounds turn all at once and then follow the first mover *toward*; the herd has no first mover and goes *away*. Whatever reached thirty animals at once, round a shoulder, ahead of the sound, is the thing seven hounds turned to. Same sense, opposite answer. Pike's word was *loud*. **"Derived. Not tested."** Lira asks whether a point the size of a seed would test it. **Protected: "On a slope, flight is a landslide with legs."** **"Write it in yours, too. I'd like it in two hands."** He writes it in the log. Growth: he learns a category his own catalogue lacked (derived, entered as such) and what it is to be asked to hold someone else's finding.
    69	5. **The night of the 75th.** Brom on what he is carrying; Lira on running as work.
    70	
    71	## Chapter 18 — the narrowings and the eighth (days 76–77)
    72	
    73	1. **Narrowings one to five (day 76).** The numbers; the cart's gauge against each; Brom against the strap where scree crosses the shelf; the herd keeping pace overhead.
    74	2. **Six and seven (day 77).** The seventh at five feet nine: a hand to spare either side over the hubs. Anchor asks at the outer rim. He holds, and measures with the cord a second time.
    75	3. **The eighth.** Wide enough, and broken clean across. The slot; a stone dropped. Brom decides, since loads are his: it wants carrying. The boxes handed over. He gets his back under the axle and takes the cart across in one stride, with Seln and Cael on the shafts; Iron Skin starts at his wrists and is put down. The tail-board does not bang because Lira and Karis each have a hand on it, and nobody told them to. The line for the eighth says a carter alone does not cross it.
    76	4. **The bay and the ledge in sight.** Seln is already under an overhang by a seep at the bay's east end. He has looked at the fin: the shale has gone round that rock before; he would have slept there if it had water. The ledge, level and narrow, running to the shoulder. Lira: **"I'll run it at first light."** The herd is on its shelf above the bay. **The log, ending on the protected Ch5-close paragraph.**
    77	
    78	## Chapter 19 — the ledge and the stampede (day 78)
    79	
    80	Positions at first light: the cart drawn out onto the scree apron thirty yards east of the fin, to wait for the ledge; Seln and Cael at the shafts; Brom on the strap, up-slope; Karis at the off wheel with the cord; Lira on the ledge, eighty yards out. The herd on its shelf, three hundred and fifty yards up. Objectives: five people and three boxes alive on the far side of what comes down. Limits: no declaration but the one that cannot be helped; Cael's calf; Brom's wrist; Lira's ankle.
    81	
    82	1. **First light.** Frost. Lira goes out; he writes the number of the first ledge-hundred and does not count her strides.
    83	2. **The ledge goes.** A length of it peels off under her; her body bursts before she chooses, and throws her back onto sound rock. The crack crosses the gorge and comes back late. **Thirty heads come up**, all at once. They go east along their shelf, away from the burst, and the shelf will not hold them at a run; it sheds, and they come down the face in a front eighty yards wide.
    84	3. **"There. Thirty seconds. Lee side."** Cael, pointing at the fin. Seln takes Karis. Brom, out of the strap, looks at the scree creeping round the wheels: **"Cart's gone."** He drops the tail-board and takes two boxes, one to a shoulder; the wrist goes dark under the cloth.
    85	4. **Karis at the nose.** The fin is a yard thick and thirty animals are coming; it will not turn them alone. She has a derived finding and nothing else. She begins to lay points up the face, points first, joins last. The third box is still on the cart. Cael goes back for it.
    86	5. **The line.** A chevron of fire across the face, its apex eleven paces above the nose: a declaration the size of a road. The front splits at eleven paces. He is eight yards short, on the east side, with a box on his right shoulder. **Compression and Anchor want.** He prices both: either is a second declaration, on the lee side of her east arm, and animals caught between two loud things do not choose well; the split stands on her line and one more loud thing turns it. He holds. A plated shoulder takes him raw: **three ribs, left side, low; the calf opened on the shale.** He keeps the box. Brom and Seln drag him in.
    87	6. **Eight seconds.** She sits down at the eighth, emptied; the last of them are by. **The cart floats** on the moving apron, turns once and goes into the gorge with its shafts up; the sound comes back late. Things heavier than a cart are heard to fall as well: **heard, not sworn.** Four in the hollow. He finds Lira three strides short of it on the west side, with her right leg under a plate.
    88	7. **Lira's window (≈1,800; two scenes).** From first light: running as work; the ankle a beat behind; the ledge going; **the burst her body chose before she did**; the landing; her own crack coming back to her off the far wall; thirty heads; knowing whose noise it was. The run in: Wind says *now* at every stride and she refuses it, since a second burst is a second shout. The road of fire across the mountain. The plate. Lying under it while the west stream goes by. The boy's face over her, grey. **Protected: "That was me," … "The loud thing."** / "It was the ledge. The ledge went. Then it was you." / "Then it was me."
    89	
    90	## Chapter 20 — the fin's lee and the healer (days 78–80)
    91	
    92	1. **The plate lifted.** Brom's back and Seln's lever; her foot comes out of the boot and the boot goes with the scree. The leg splinted between two thin plates of shale with strips of canvas. Inventory: Cael's ribs and calf; Brom's wrist open under the cloth; Karis sitting where she sat down. Three boxes. What they have to eat is what was on their backs.
    93	2. **Day 78, night.** Karis takes the flint and steel to the lantern and cannot make her hands do it; she could not set a point if the mountain asked. Brom lights it. No blankets. The herd in twos and threes on the lower slope, spacing itself again.
    94	3. **Day 79.** Cold rain. The fan above them still sliding, a plate at a time, through the day and the night. Seln crosses it for water, and goes west along the ledge and walks the lip, and scratches a mark on each stone that bears. Lira's leg hot under the plates. A loaf and a half. Nobody writes: Cael opens the book once and shuts it.
    95	4. **Day 80, midday: her walk.** A mule on the ledge, and a woman on its down-slope side, at a carter's pace. A crossbow in a cover on the pack. She stops a stride and a half off. **Protected: "Who's worst?"** … **"Name's Oryn."** … **"Tide. Healer."** … **"Iron. Four, if anybody's keeping a book up here."** She is the first healer any of them has met past the Line, and Lira knows who she must be: the one who walks a route.
    96	5. **The set and the mend.** The plates off. "That's the part you'll mind." Brom holds Lira's shoulders. Then both hands on the leg, for less than an hour; a count of ten first, under her breath (the surface reading). Lira: "Cold." … "No. It's going along." Oryn sits as a carter sets down a known load. **Protected: "That's one,"** … **"Hands on. That's the only way it works."** … "And I can't do it to me." (the heel).
    97	6. **The wrist; the empty one.** "Then it should have closed in a month and hasn't, because you've pulled on it." Two on a road. Karis read at a count of ten: "Ember. And nothing. You're empty." That fills or it does not; it is not hers to mend. The scorch across the face; who did it; how long. "Hm." To Seln: the marks on the lip were good.
    98	7. **The first failed surface reading.** Her hands on his ribs; she stops in the middle, looks at her hands, tries again; her face does what a brass plate did once. **The protected exchange, ending on "That's a *finding*."** The four behind him go very still. She binds the calf in her own cloth. They eat her bread. **The log (protected Ch6 entry).**
    99	
   100	## Chapter 21 — tape, the lip and the walk down (days 81–83)
   101	
   102	1. **Tape (day 81, first light).** Strips of gummed linen, three, laid from breastbone to spine. "It tells the ribs where the edge is." Why tape for him and hands for Lira: she mends what she can read. He lets somebody else price a hurt of his.
   103	2. **The lip, thirty-one paces (hazard).** Seln's marks. Oryn takes the mule over first with two boxes, herself on the outside. Lira next, riding, with the new leg hanging. Brom with the third box on his back: "Carrying." / "Not pulling." Karis. Cael last: three ribs and a breath that stops at a certain depth; he counts thirty-one. Anchor asks at every step and is refused.
   104	3. **The first day down.** Her walk catalogued: the pace; the down-slope side; the heel, raw, taped, because she cannot heal herself. Lira on a mule, not mentioning the mule.
   105	4. **The ankle refused (day 82).** Oryn has Lira's foot in her hands to see the leg; Lira asks once. The arithmetic: two on a road; a table she is five days late to; an old hurt already knit that would have to be undone first. **Protected: "That's a tape and a season. It'll set."** Lira does not ask twice. Oryn shows her how to tape it properly, and makes her do it herself.
   106	5. **The second night's fire: the route.** Karis asks to see her book. There is none. **Protected: "Twenty days," … "Round. …"**; seven holds; thirty-odd rounds; late eleven times and now twelve; what each owes her (a bed, the mule shod, a coat once) and what she owes; "That's what late is. It's not a number. It's who I don't know about."; **protected: "I remember who I've had my hands on," … "That's the book. …"**; the city's list, kept by tier; **protected: the want ("In the city I healed Bronzes with bruises. …")**. Lira, who asked what she was becoming with nobody measuring, listens to an Iron of the fourth rank who walked away from the list.
   107	6. **Day 83.** What a wrapped wrist allows (a week: what sits on it, never what hangs from it). The valley road; the last halting-ground; Oxhollow's smoke.
   108	
   109	## Chapter 22 — Oxhollow (day 84), and two windows
   110	
   111	1. **The ford and the yard.** A wide gravel ford; the hold on the north bank, in a hollow, with a gate; a yoke of oxen for drawing carts through. The lean-to against the gate-house, and a scrubbed table that is hers twenty days at a time. People waiting on a bench. The hold feeds her first, and she sleeps two hours before she touches anybody (fed and slept).
   112	2. **The map paid.** At the ford-keeper's house, while she sleeps. Sixty-three lines and what came after the last of them. **Protected: "Then they go round," … "That's worth seventy to know before the first cart's in the gorge. I'd sooner pay it for this."** Seventy counted out; Oxhollow's mark on the slip. A hundred and sixteen in hand; six owed.
   113	3. **The table.** Worst first: she walks the bench with a count of ten on each. A scald gone bad; a foot an ox has stood on; a collarbone. "That's three." The rest are taped, told, or put off till the next round. **The stall**, named once, when Lira asks what Tide does in a fight: a limit she prices and hates; not shown.
   114	4. **The second and third readings.** The ribs: knitting, with "nothing doing it". The wrist and the neck, where nothing is wrong: still nothing. **Protected: "It's like putting my hands in a river in the dark. …"** The two readings taught: surface, a count of ten; deep, both hands, minutes, the patient still and whole, at a mending's cost; "I don't run it on a road". **Protected: "When you're not broken, I run the long one. …"**
   115	5. **Three questions.** Did they knit in the right places (yes). Slower than other people or faster (faster). Has any healer ever got anything from him (no). "That's the visit." He has a sentence asking her south and does not say it. **Protected: "Thornwater's on my route in nine days. …" / "Nine days."** She goes out by the gate, north, at dusk.
   116	6. **Oryn's window (≈2,500).** Out of the gate; four people and their mules coming down the north road, one with an old limp she does not owe. The arithmetic of twelve times late. The hand she owed: a carter at the foot of the lip with a split hand, promised that evening and left with a rag on it; he hauls to Thornwater. The hold that gave the coat. The child three stops on. Each night her hands, and a patient she wants to read whole. On the road on the ninety-second she puts a day on the route. It ends before Thornwater. No theory; no other hold named.
   117	7. **Teague's window (≈1,350; day 84, dusk).** The healer's mule passed on the north road. His three counted. The yard read through the gate in passing: no cart; a girl with one boot; a box on a big man's back; the mild one, found first this time. What they have cost him. Four things to say, each priced before he says it.
   118	8. **The bank.** **Protected: Teague's lines and Cael's "Yes."** Cael has no argument. **The log (protected Ch7 entry).** Teague's four walking the ford quiet; and a mule a mile up the north road with a woman on its down-slope side.
   119	
   120	## Windows (planned)
   121	
   122	Lira ≈1,800 (ch19, day 78). Oryn ≈2,500 (ch22, days 84–92). Teague ≈1,350 (ch22, day 84). Cael the rest.
   123	
   124	## Withheld (checked against the packet)
   125	
   126	No fragment is used on the mountain or anywhere in the movement; deployment since the Line stays one. Compression and Anchor want and are refused. Shadow sealed. Oryn learns nothing of fragments; no deep reading is run; she records nothing. The herd's flight is derived, not tested, and is entered so; the herd's dead are heard, not sworn. The leg is mended; the ankle is not. Nobody mentions quiet ground. Oryn's other five holds are not named. Teague learns nothing he could not read off a yard; his crew is the spear, the bow and the boy. No new name for any person, place or animal. No season, month or weekday names; no year count since the Kindling; Cael is eighteen; never "his Path" of Cael. Oryn's age is not stated. Karis's stride and the whole length of her cord are not stated.
   127	
   128	## Amendments made while drafting (recorded after the prose; the list above is as first written)
   129	
   130	- **The fin's geometry.** The list had the fin running down the fall line with a "nose" uphill. As drafted it lies **square across the fall of the ground**, like a wall across a field, halfway between the foot of the face and the rim: as long as a barn, a yard or so thick, waist-high at either end and twice a man's height in the middle, with shale banked chest-high along its upper side and a strip of bare floor twelve feet deep on its lower (lee) side. The wall stops stone; it would not have stopped thirty animals, which is why Karis's line is needed. Karis stands at its low eastern end. Her chevron's apex is eleven paces above the wall's middle; each arm runs down and outward to a little beyond the wall's end: **nine points and eight channels, about sixty yards from end to end.**
   131	- **Who goes back for what.** All five reach the lee first. Karis says that the wall will stop the stone and not the herd. Brom looks back at the cart, says the packet line, and *then* runs back for two boxes. Cael goes after the third while she is laying points. The tail-board bangs this time, with nobody there to stop it.
   132	- **Cael counts her set points** by the small dip of her hand and the direction of her eyes, as he has watched her lay them for two years. No fragment perceives anything. (A first draft gave this to the Ember fragment; it was taken out, so that nothing of his answers on the mountain, even passively.)
   133	- **Where the shoulder takes him:** eight yards short of the wall's end and outside the foot of her eastern arm, in the edge of the eastern stream. He gives it his left side, as Lira told him at the ford, and is thrown toward the wall with the box.
   134	- **The ledge.** The part that goes is the middle of it, from about eighty yards out, for thirty-one paces; the ledge is sound beyond that to the shoulder. **The lip** is the scar where that part was rooted: four feet wide, tilted outward, loose with plates. Seln scratches a mark on each stone that bears: **forty-four marks.**
   135	- **The cart was drawn out onto the bay's frozen shale at first light** on Brom's reasoning (the frost glues the plates for an hour). It stands thirty yards east of the wall when the ledge goes.
   136	- **The fan haul** is in **six** stages, not four, since the ferryman's rope will not reach farther between rock islands. The rope runs over a peeled log bedded in a notch (Brom's), not over bare rock. Karis signals with her arms (one up, walk; both, hold).
   137	- **The hundreds** reach sixty-two at the bay. Line sixty-three is the ledge, written by Karis on the eighty-second evening: *Sixty-three. The ledge. Gone, for thirty-one paces. Cart: no.*
   138	- **Seln's report at the fan's foot** carries two tales from Pike: a salt string on the cold road whose Force shifted a block with his Path under a ledge herd; and roofing-stone cutters who once came up this same side water with carts and stopped coming. The wheel-ruts on the track are theirs.
   139	- **Karis's day, the fourth observation:** she starts a count when the plate tips, sees both ends of the line step in the same instant, and has said *one* and begun *two* before the sound reaches her. Cael, watching the westernmost animal beyond a rib of the hill, hears its own plates before he hears the slide. Brom's objection (the ground shaking) is answered by the noon rock, which struck the herd's own shelf and moved two. Lira supplies the word "frightened".
   140	- **The night of the seventy-fifth** became its own short scene: Seln back with a report of the eight narrowings, the bay and the ledge; Brom's "So it needn't be on purpose"; Lira's promise to run under the wall.
   141	- **Seln goes down for help at first light on the eightieth** and meets the healer already on the track below the shoulder; he walks in front of her mule across the lip. She has come because a carter at the foot of the hill saw the mountain come down and knew from the plank that a crew had gone up it.
   142	- **Day 79 has no entry and day 78 has none.** The log resumes on the eightieth with the protected entry.
   143	- **Oryn's rule for Brom** is given on the morning of the eighty-first, with the wrap: a week from the mending; "It'll take what sits on it. It won't take what hangs from it." The packet's "Carrying." / "Not pulling." is at the lip: Brom says the first and Oryn the second, agreeing.
   144	- **The lip, crossing order:** Seln; Oryn and the mule with two boxes (and back); the mule with Lira up; Brom with the third box on his back; Seln back for Karis; Cael last. At the twentieth pace an unmarked slab that looks better than the marked stone turns over behind him.
   145	- **The herd is counted at twenty-five** from the western ledge on the eighty-first. That is not entered as five dead.
   146	- **The ankle** is refused at the noon halt on the eighty-second; the route is told at that night's fire, on the valley road. **The map is paid at the end of chapter 21** (the eighty-fourth morning, while the healer eats and sleeps); chapter 22 opens on her table.
   147	- **Oxhollow's table:** a scald gone bad, an ox-driver's foot, a boy's collarbone; five others looked at and sent away. The stall is named once, in answer to Lira.
   148	- **Oryn's window** is in two scenes (the hill out of Oxhollow, where she passes Teague's four; then the eighty-sixth to the ninety-second). The hold that gave the coat is the next north of Oxhollow; the child is at the gate after it and is no worse. The carter's hand is owed at Thornwater. She has added a stop three times before (the fifth, sixth and seventh holds).
   149	- **What they have cost Teague:** he had timed his second slip to meet the healer's round at the upper hold on the eighty-first, for the bow's hip, and waited a day, and she did not come. He learns why from her own mouth on the hill and from Oxhollow's yard and keeper.
   150	- **Windows as drafted:** Lira about 1,900 words in two scenes (chapter 19); Oryn about 2,100 in two (chapter 22); Teague about 1,250 in one (chapter 22).
   151	- **Rhythm:** chapters 17, 20 and 21 measured short in first draft (means 11.0–12.0) and were re-composed by reading in the same session (17 and 21 rewritten whole); chapters 15, 16, 18 and 19 had too many sentences of forty words or more and were parted by hand. A vocabulary pass then put the series' own words (Path, fragment, declaration, reserve, mending, the surface and deep readings) where plainer ones had stood.

