editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-55-"No," Cael agreed, and set his left forearm across his chest and braced it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:57:He had been thinking about the full push since the day the half came. The half had come not from reaching harder but from choosing a wider road before the force arrived, a channel cut for twice the water. A whole slow push would need the whole road, as broad as Brom's arm, laid out back along the line the push would come in and held there, steady, the whole time the force was arriving. If he reached for more than he had chosen, or chose the whole and then flinched from the size of it, it would go into a joint. The size had to be decided first and then not changed, and that was the entire difficulty, because nothing in him wanted to stand still while a thing that large came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-59-Brom knelt and laid his palm on the forearm and let the Iron Skin come up under it, and leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-61-The first three tries did nothing; he had chosen the whole road and then, in the instant before reaching, narrowed it without meaning to, the way a hand closes a little on a rope when the weight comes on. The fourth caught a quarter, out of old habit, and cost what a quarter cost. The fifth he narrowed again. On the sixth he caught himself narrowing and opened the road again in the same breath, and the force slid sideways off it and went into his left elbow with a deep sick throb, and he sat back on his heels with the arm folded against him.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-143-"How long?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-145-"Until I can't lay a clean point. Then a little longer, so that we know what not clean looks like." She closed the ledger. "Saturday. After the midday meal. Bring something to eat; you'll be standing a long time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:147:It ran most of the afternoon. Brom came for the first hour and Lira for the second, and for the third there was nobody but the two of them in the whitewashed room with the high window going from white to gold to grey. Karis laid single points and paired points and channels. The thin singing lines of her Drawn Channel ran between pairs of coins of light laid on the floor and the whitewashed walls, at every length the room allowed. She held points to counts of twenty and forty and sixty, and once, near the end of the second hour, to a count of a hundred. It was a steady small sun the size of a coin, hanging in the air at the height of his heart, while she stood perfectly still behind it with her off hand motionless and her face going slowly pale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-149-He watched, and reached, every way he knew, in turn, and again, and nothing happened at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-151-But something else did, in the third hour, and he almost missed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:153:He was at three paces. They had come in closer in the last hour, because closer was the only variable left that day that they had not run long. He had opened the read, the Iron-adjacent pressure-sense along his skin, not because it had any business with Ember but because he had begun to open everything he had, every channel of attention at once, on the theory that if something was going to come in, it might come in by a door he was not watching. The read had nothing to do. There was no weight in the air between them to tell him anything. Karis stood still and her points bloomed and died, and the read lay open on the surface of his skin like a hand on a still pond.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-155-Then, a breath before one of her points bloomed, the air at the place where it would bloom was heavier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-157-It was so faint he did not believe it. A density, a thickening, in that one small place, as the felt on the training post had thickened before Brom shifted. A breath later the point bloomed there, exactly there, a dim coin of light where the heaviness had been.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-191-They ran the thirteenth and fourteenth on the last day of the tenth week, two short ones, a quarter-hour each, one at dawn and one before supper, at five paces, because Karis was still cold at the fingertips from the long afternoon. Both were null.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-193-That evening Karis read the ledger back to him in the archive, at Prynn's long table. She read all fourteen sessions in order, as the fourth clause said she must before any of it existed anywhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:195:She read it in her flat even voice, the voice she used for timings, and he listened to fourteen sessions of his own failure set down in another person's handwriting. *Distance nine paces. Distance one. Source's back turned. Declarations unannounced. Source tired. Witness eating. Witness withholding pen. Duration three hours and some minutes.* And after every one of them, the same word, written the same size, with the same care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-197-*Null.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md-199-He waited to feel what he had always felt, all his life, when somebody read him back a list of the things he could not do. It did not come. He had been studied his whole life by people for whom his failures proved something they had already decided about him, assessors and clerks and a patient official in a district office somewhere. When those people wrote down what he could not do, they were adding a stone to a wall that had been going up since before he was born. Karis wrote down what he could not do as if each failure were a stake driven into a field she was surveying, a small exact mark that said *the edge is not here, look further on*. Even his nothing, in her hand, deserved to be got exactly right. Nobody had ever told him, in all the years of being written down, that a person could be measured and feel kept company by it.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-15-She got up and washed and dressed and sat down at the little table by her window with both notebooks, because that was what she did with mornings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-17-The first notebook was easy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:19:She wrote the match into it as she would have written any bout: the conditions, the panel, the three exchanges, her point counts in each, the time she held them through the resets, the sustain cost that had come due afterward in her fingertips and gone by morning. She wrote that the candidate had read her near points before they lit, and that she had moved her floor outward in the second exchange to answer it. She wrote that he had crossed a channel, the shortest one, and that she had not believed he would. She wrote that her fourth channel's last point had failed to take, because the place she had laid it was already burning, and that the assembly had lost its sequence. She wrote, because it was true and hers to record, that she had been holding every lit channel on that count, and that when the count broke she had lost her hold on all of them at once, and they had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-21-Then she stopped, with her pen over the page, at the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-23-She did not write *by what*. Clause five of the first terms forbade it, and clause two of the second, in Brom's square capitals, forbade it twice over; she had agreed to both and written one of them herself, and she would not have written it anyway. There were things she did not put on paper because a rule said she must not, and there were things she did not put on paper because she would not, and since yesterday this was both.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-27-The ledger was harder. It was the ledger of the nulls, twenty-two sessions in her small upright hand with the ruled line under them and the sentence about the missing condition. It recorded conditions and results and nothing else, and a match was not a session; it had not been run in the quiet room, or under her consent spoken aloud, or by any rule she had written. But it had been the twenty-third time she had stood in front of him while he watched, and she could not leave the ledger without it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-29-So she wrote it under the barley clause, under everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:31:*Supplementary. Not a session. Formal yard, fifth sitting, full intensity, under the presiding assessor's conditions. Source: K., Ember Path, Iron Rank Three. Consent: written, filed. Distance: from eight paces to one. Duration: three exchanges. Result, as reported by the subject: a fragment notice. Ember-adjacent. The field reads: acquisition, directed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-33-She looked at it for a long time, the first entry in the ledger that did not end in *null*. Her hand did not want to write anything under it, and she let her hand have its way, and closed the ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md-35-Then she opened the second notebook, the one that held everything the standard model got wrong. She turned to a clean page, and wrote the date at the top, and did not write anything else for an hour.

