editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:161:The third was that evening, after supper, at five paces. Karis worked in pairs, two points at a time a pace apart, held to a count of twenty and let go together. Cael tried invitation, with his eyes shut between each pair and open for the bloom, the space held open in him beside the four like a hand held out across a room. Nothing came of it. *Session three,* the ledger said. *Source: Iron Rank Three, Ember, demonstration intensity. Distance five paces. Declarations: paired ignition points, held to count twenty. Subject's reach: invitation, eyes closed between declarations. Notice: none. Result: null.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:163:The fourth was on Sunday morning, before the first bell, with nobody else in the quiet room at all. Karis had asked whether he wanted to try it close, and he had said yes, and they had argued a little about how close. She settled it by walking up to a pace from him and saying, "Here. If it's too near, I'll know before you do." From one pace he could feel the heat of her points on his face. Each one bloomed a hand's width from his cheek, steady as a coin of lamplight, and the warmth came across his skin like standing near a hearth with his eyes shut. He wanted, plainly and with his whole chest, as hard as he had ever wanted anything. Nothing happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:87:The first point bloomed on the earth a pace to his right, where the framework would have sent him, and the second went down a pace behind his left heel. He saw them both. He saw her off hand go still before each, and he saw in the same instant that the stillness was not a quarter of a second. It was a tenth: the same tell, in the same place, cut down to almost nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:It came up along his skin as it always did, the readiness along his forearms and the backs of his hands and the front of his body. It was the weight-read he had taken from Brom and taught to find a body's lean through linen and felt and an oak post. In the quiet room, at three paces, it had begun to do something more: a thickening in the air at the place where her next point would bloom, a breath before it bloomed. Three times in twelve. Twice in the last session. Never more. But in the quiet room every point had been a demonstration, laid for a ledger with nothing behind it, and he had never once had the read open inside a real fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:143:It was a single small flicker across her face, there and gone, the researcher in her noting a reading that did not fit, at the speed of a fight. She did not stop or hesitate. She changed. The next three points did not go down near him at all; they went down far off, at the edges of the ring, four and five and six paces from him, well beyond where any read could feel the air lean. She had worked out from the way he moved that he could feel her points coming when they were close, and in the space of two steps she had moved her whole floor out to where he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:147:A channel ran across the ring from one far point to another, seven paces long, singing, and swept the floor between them in half, and another lit beside it. The far points needed no reading once they were lit; he could see them as well as anyone on the benches. They were boxing the middle of the ring into narrower and narrower lanes. Inside the lanes her near points still bloomed close, and the read still gave him those half a breath early, but the long lines were coming from where he could not feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:151:Between him and her, two paces off, a short channel ran between two near points. It was the shortest line on the floor, barely the length of his own reach, and the one her drawing least expected anybody to challenge, because no one in his right mind walks through fire to get nearer the person lighting it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:171:It was one honest reach, the open palm he had laid on Edran's chest, and for the first time in the match she gave ground. She went back a full step, fast, and then another, and he saw the whole floor convulse round its maker. Her near points went out in a rush of shimmer and two long channels died, released, while she laid new points as she went back, three of them, in a tight curve behind her, three paces off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:He knew it as he had known the south chalk at his heels in the first exchange. The wall of fire on his left was closing round toward his back. Ahead of him, a pace off, the fourth channel of the true assembly was forming, the one that would shut the last gap. He could feel its first point already lit on the far side, and its second about to bloom on the earth a pace in front of his right foot, the air leaning there, heavy, sure, half a breath ahead of the light. When that point bloomed the fourth channel would join to it, and the whole assembly would light round him in a single instant, with a sound like an indrawn breath. There would be nowhere he could stand, and she would not need to touch him, and Wray would say *halt*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:235:Most of his decisions were weighed first and turned over and only then set down in the square hand, afterward. This one was not like those. He was awake for it. That was the difference, the whole difference, as being awake is the whole difference between falling and diving. Something in him that had stood in the quiet room twenty-two times, reaching and watching itself reach, was not watching anything now. There was nothing left over to watch with. There was only Karis, trying with everything she had, and a point about to bloom a pace in front of his foot, and him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:23:She came out at once, before he had settled, two long strides out of her own grey onto clean ground. She went straight at him with nothing slowing anybody down at all, and his fast hand flicked out and caught her on the shoulder, exactly where it had caught her at the review, and she took it. Cael saw her take it. He saw her see the hand coming, and see the space where she could have turned from it, and not turn. She let it land on the point of her shoulder, where everyone at the rail and all three chairs could see it land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:87:He knew her now. That was what he understood, standing three paces from her in the last hour of the series while the first point bloomed at the height of her shoulder and held. He knew her better than he had known anybody he had ever watched, better than he had known Reydan at the top of the bout and better, in this one narrow way, than he knew Lira. He knew where she would lay the next point before she laid it. Twice in the hour, with the read open, he felt the air at that place grow faintly heavier before the light came, as it had on the long afternoon. He could have shut his eyes and rebuilt any one of twenty-two sessions from the sounds alone, the small hiss of a point, the kettle-song of a channel, the click of her pen. He had learned her past memory, down into the place below memory where Lira's footwork lived in his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:*Session one. Source: K., Ember Path, Iron Rank Three. Intensity: demonstration. Consent given aloud. Distance nine paces. Duration one hour. Declarations: single ignition points; paired points; one Drawn Channel, laid on the end wall; one sustained point, count thirty. Subject's reach: attention, invitation, want, rebuilt stillness, in that order. Fragment notice: none. Result: null. No change in source.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-61.md:31:*Iron-adjacent. Brom. The surface read, from contact out to a few paces, thinner the faster they come. It read through linen and quilting and felt; it gave me Karis's points as weight before they were light. For most of a year I tried to make it be something else. It's better at being what it is.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:141:That was all she did in the whole first exchange. Two points. A great deal of footwork. When Wray called the end of it, Karis was a pace from the south chalk and Edran was in the middle of the ring with the crowd murmuring, and anyone who had come to see the Ternhall transfer do something remarkable looked faintly disappointed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:153:Cael watched him see them: nine coins of light on the floor round him in a loose curve, and the Stone fourth-year standing among them with his broad calm face, understanding all at once that he had been walking into something for two exchanges. He did not panic; Hobb did not have panic in him. He stopped pressing and set his feet, and Karis, a pace beyond him, joined two of her points.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:169:The instructor dropped his hand, and Hobb set himself, and Karis, standing quite still with her off hand motionless at her side, laid four more points in the space of a breath. Then she joined everything she had, so that the channels lit across section two together, with a sound like somebody drawing breath, faint lines of heat running from point to point to point along the boards in every direction a body might want to go, too many and too fast for Cael to count before he saw what they had made. He counted sixteen points. The section had been an open floor, and now it was a drawing of a floor, and between all the lines there were exactly two places a person could stand without crossing one. She was already walking toward the nearer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-33.md:201:"That's what I felt in the quiet room. At three paces. Before her points."

