   253	That was how it seemed. She had been running a hand's breadth from it, and between one stride and the next there was a gap there the width of her arm, with daylight in it. The shelf under her had come off the mountain in one piece and was leaning out.
   254	
   255	She did not decide anything.
   256	
   257	She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
   258	
   259	The air broke round her. She felt it in her teeth.
   260	
   261	Then there was rock under both feet, and she was standing on it, twenty feet back, with her arms out and her heart going like a mill. Behind her the ledge roared down into the gorge. She had not chosen where to come down, either. Her feet had found sound stone under the roof by themselves, and stopped dead on it, as she had spent a month on a river bar teaching them to stop.
   262	
   263	*I'm alive*, she thought, with real surprise.
   264	
   265	Then the far wall answered her.
   266	
   267	It was her own burst, coming home. It crossed three hundred yards of empty air and came back from the cliff late and clean, and it did not sound like a thing she had done. It sounded like a whip in somebody else's hand.
   268	
   269	She understood it then, in the pit of her stomach, before she had turned her head.
   270	
   271	There was a page with a comma struck out of it. She had been lying on a slab when it was written. *It will answer a declaration in the same way.* She had asked for a test, a seed, half a breath; and a small pale person had looked up a hill and declined.
   272	
   273	She looked up the hill.
   274	
   275	Thirty heads were lifted, all of them, turned away from her.
   276	
   277	---
   278	
   279	She ran.
   280	
   281	There was nothing else to do with what she had done. The cart was a toy on the white floor of the bay, with four specks beside it, and one of the specks had its arm out. She could not hear what it said. She did not need to, for the arm was pointing at the long pale rock.
   282	
   283	It was sixty yards of ledge to the bay and forty more of shale to the wall's near end, and she had run farther than that to catch a dog.
   284	
   285	Wind said *now*.
   286	
   287	It said it at the second stride, quite reasonably. It had just saved her life and it could not see why it was not wanted again. Twenty feet at a time, five times, and she would be behind that stone before the first of them was halfway down.
   288	
   289	*No.*
   290	
   291	She knew what a second one was. She had sat in a loft and heard it laid out with a charred stick. *Never two at once.* A burst would crack over that bay like the first, and thirty animals that were running from one loud thing would have another to run from, and it would be going off beside the only shelter on the mountain. She had been the loud thing once this morning without being asked. She would not be it a second time by choice.
   292	
   293	She refused it at the third stride and the fourth. It was harder than it had been at the ford, where she had only been afraid of a dog.
   294	
   295	The right foot came down late. She allowed for it. The ledge poured back under her, and the hill on her left was no longer a hill; it was brown, and moving, and the sound of it came down ahead of it and pressed on her ears. She came off the rock onto the shale and it broke under her like the crust on snow.
   296	
   297	Thirty yards.
   298	
   299	A small figure stood at the wall's far end with its hands out. She saw it across the whole length of the pale rock, no bigger than a doll, and she knew whose hands they were.
   300	
   301	The mountain caught fire.
   302	
   303	She had watched those points go down in barns and annexes and on the end wall of a whitewashed room, a dozen at a time, each no bigger than a barley grain. She had seen them joined in threes, and once, at a sitting, sixteen. She had never seen a road. It stood up across the whole face above the rock, two straight burning lines that met in a point high on the slope, as wide as a street and as long as a field; and the animals coming down on it split like a log under a wedge.
   304	
   305	*She's staked the whole page on it*, thought Lira, running. *With us underneath.*
   306	
   307	Twenty yards. The near arm of the fire came down the slope ahead of her and stopped a little beyond the wall's end, and on this side of it, on her side, the right-hand stream was coming.
   308	
   309	She was outside the line. She saw that with great clearness. Ten yards more would put her under the rock, and she did not have ten yards; she had perhaps six, and a foot that was a beat behind.
   310	
   311	*Now*, said Wind, for the last time, and it was not reasonable any longer. It was begging.
   312	
   313	She told it no, and ran the six yards on her feet.
   314	
   315	Something the size of a cart went behind her so close that the wind of it turned her half round. She kept her legs. She was three strides from the stone, with her arm already out for it, when the shale that the thing had brought down with it took her ankles from under her like a wave on a beach, and she went onto her face.
   316	
   317	She felt the plate come.
   318	
   319	It was not quick. It slid up over her calves on the moving stone as a lid slides onto a box, and settled, and the weight of the hill behind it leaned. Her left leg was in a hollow and it only pinned. Her right was lying across something hard.
   320	
   321	It went below the knee, with a duller sound than the ankle had made at a river. She knew at once that it was a different thing.
   322	
   323	After that she lay with her cheek on the mountain and watched the stream go by. It took a long time and no time. Grey backs passed at the level of her eyes, and stones ran over her outstretched hand without hurting it, and none of it seemed to have much to do with her. The fire went out. She noticed that, and thought, *She's done*, and was sorry.
   324	
   325	Then it was quiet, and the gorge was giving something back.
   326	
   327	She could not have said how long it was before the boy came. He came along the lower side of the rock, on his feet and hardly so, with one arm shut against his ribs and his left leg dragging; and he was the colour of the dust. He got down beside her by letting the wall take him. His eyes went over her a piece at a time, as they always did. They reached the plate, and stopped.
   328	
   329	She had one thing to say, and this time she did not pick it with any care.
   330	
   331	"That was me," said Lira. Her mouth was full of grit. "The loud thing."
   332	
   333	He did not tell her that it was not. She watched him not tell her. He had never once made her a present of a thing that was untrue, at a fire or anywhere, and she saw him go through what had happened in its order, as he would have gone through a bout.
   334	
   335	"It was the ledge. The ledge went. Then it was you."
   336	
   337	She turned that over, with her face on the stone. It had the ledge in it, first, where the ledge belonged. It had her in it second, and it did not leave her out.
   338	
   339	"Then it was me."

