   152	
   153	She walked at a carter's pace. It was the pace that he had fallen in behind on the fifth day, which is neither quick nor slow and can be kept from dark to dark; she did not lengthen it when the bay opened in front of her, or when she saw four people lying under a rock. She had one hand laid on the mule's shoulder. She kept herself on the side where the drop was and the beast on the side where the wall was, and when the ledge widened she did not change over.
   154	
   155	She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder and broad in the hand, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more.
