   159	She brought the mule across the shale to the end of the wall and stopped it with a word, a stride and a half from where Lira lay. Her eyes went over the five of them a single time, and it did not take her as long as it takes to count five. He saw her eyes go to the slates on a leg, and to a dark cloth at a wrist, and to the way that he himself was sitting, with one arm shut against his side; and they rested longest on Karis, who had nothing wrong with her that could be seen.
   160	
   161	"Who's worst?"
   162	
   163	It was a low voice, and a little hoarse, like a voice that was not much used on the road.
   164	
   165	She had not asked who they were, or what had happened to them, or how five people came to be lying under a rock at the top of a mountain with three boxes. Cael noticed that. He had been met at gates and counters and tables for as long as he could remember, and at every one of them the first question had been what he was; this woman had walked a day and a half up a hill to put a different one, and it was the only one she appeared to want answered.
   166	
   167	"Her," said Brom, and pointed at Lira.
   168	
   169	"I'm fine," said Lira.
   170	
   171	"You're not. I can see your leg from here." The woman was already kneeling. She had Cael's coat sleeve unknotted in the time it took to say it, and she lifted off the upper slate and looked at what was under it without touching. "Who put these on?"
   172	
   173	"I did," said Brom.
   174	
   175	"They've kept it. That's all they were asked to do." She glanced up at Lira's face, as one looks at the head of a horse before going round behind it. "Name's Oryn."
   176	
   177	"Are you a healer?" said Lira. Her teeth were knocking together. "A woman at a gate said there wasn't one. She said the nearest walked a route."
   178	
   179	"Tide. Healer."
   180	
   181	"She said the rest of you keep to the country where somebody ranks you."
   182	
   183	"Most do."
   184	
   185	"Do they rank you?"
   186	
   187	The woman had laid two fingers on the shin, a hand's breadth below the knee, as lightly as on the string of an instrument.
   188	
   189	"Iron. Four, if anybody's keeping a book up here."
