     1	# Chapter 5 — Route
     2	
     3	The slip was in a hold's hand, and the hold was one he had never heard of.
     4	
     5	He read it at the plank on the seventieth morning past the Line, in the order the board taught, which was every slip once and then the ones that paid twice: *Oxhollow ford. High traverse to Thornwater wanted scouted before the autumn carts. Shale bench above the gorge; two days under the void road; not walked three seasons, since the slide. Map wanted, and a word on whether it holds a cart. Seventy marks on the map, paid at Oxhollow.*
     6	
     7	Oxhollow was a ford, north, a week by the void road; that was the whole of what the plank knew about it.
     8	
     9	Thirteen slips. Two of them, the good two, wore Pike's HOLD and Teague's name across the top in the same pencil; the crew had been in off the autumn road and out again with the hold slips while the five of them were at the well on the south road. That left eleven: one hold's, four escorts at the carters' price, and fences and wells and a roof that wanted thatching. He sorted them by what each would cost the five of them, and the sorting put Oxhollow's slip at the top by itself before he had finished reading it the second time.
    10	
    11	He counted why, because he did not trust a thing that sorted itself.
    12	
    13	It paid. Seventy marks was the ford's price and three-quarters again, for a walk. Holds priced what a thing was worth to the hold, and a route two days under the void road was worth a great deal to a ford that sent carts south in autumn.
    14	
    15	Teague's crew was gone on the hold contracts, a week out and a week back, and Oxhollow's slip had come in on a carter behind them and sat four days on the plank with nobody's finger on it. Pike had said, to the page, when Cael asked why: "Pays on the map, at Oxhollow. That's a week north to get paid for a week's walk, and the crews that could take it are the crews with the pick. Nobody's been on the board long enough to want it."
    16	
    17	And the third reason, which he counted last because it was his: it was ground. Not teeth. A scouting run was a thing walked and measured and written down and handed to a man who would trust a cart to the writing, and he had done that every day of his life since he was fourteen, in a notebook, for nothing, at the edges of rooms where the work being paid for was somebody else's. He had never once been paid to catalogue. He read the slip a third time to be sure. It was what it said.
    18	
    19	"That one," he said.
    20	
    21	Seln, beside him, had already put a finger on it and moved it a quarter-inch on the plank.
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	Pike entered it as a price gets entered, flat, with the five names under it, and said the things he said.
    26	
    27	"Three seasons nobody's walked it. Slide came down on the Thornwater end and the carts went round by the void road and stopped talking about the bench. Oxhollow doesn't want a crew that comes back and says it's fine." The pen did not stop. "They want a map they can put a cart on. Width at every narrow. Where it holds and where it doesn't. Where you'd camp." A pause, at the end of a line. "Take a cart. Smith has one, six marks against it coming back. You want to tell a ford it holds a cart, you'd better have had one on it."
    28	
    29	He said one more thing, to Seln and not to the rest of them, when Seln stopped at the plank's end on the way out: "Ask me about the shale-backs before you go." Seln asked, there, with his back to the room, and Cael did not go to hear the answer, because a man who asked a thing quietly at the end of a plank had asked it that way on purpose.
    30	
    31	The cart was the smith's second-best, two wheels and a bed and a pair of shafts with a chest-strap between them. Brom stood in the shafts and put the strap across his chest and leaned on it once, and it moved, and he nodded, and that was the cart's whole trial. The bound arm went inside the right shaft against his ribs. The good hand took the left.
    32	
    33	The boxes went because there was nowhere to leave them. Lowmarch had no wall and a population that turned over with the season, and three boxes in a meadow belonged to whoever walked past them last. Karis roped them onto the cart's bed herself, the front box over the axle and the other two behind it, and put her hand flat on the middle lid when she was done, as if to see whether it was warm.
    34	
    35	Inventory, taken on the smith's yard on the seventy-first morning.
    36	
    37	The calf: closed, mostly. He had stopped counting on the flat, because the count had gone past twenty and a number that large was not a limp, it was a leg. Uphill it still said nine. He had not told anyone the uphill number, and there was a mountain in the slip.
    38	
    39	Brom's forearm: bound wrist to elbow still, but the wrist had closed at last, the cloth coming off clean on the sixty-sixth day, and he had looked at the closed place with the lock face and wrapped it again as if it had not. The hand was allowed to hold. It was not allowed to pull. The chest-strap pulled.
    40	
    41	Lira's ankle: the stick was gone, leaned against the inn wall on the sixty-fourth day and walked away from. What she had instead was cloth — the steading's boiled linen cut into strips, wound each morning from the arch up over the joint and tied off at the shin, tight, so that the ankle bent as far as the cloth allowed and no further, which was less than the other, and later. She called it taping. She did it before her boots, sitting, with her mouth shut.
    42	
    43	Karis's hands: two lines across each palm, pink and raised, that she flexed against the pen every morning like a woman working a hinge. The letters had come back to their size. She did not look at the palms while she wrote, and Cael did not look at them either.
    44	
    45	Five. All walking. None of it healed by anybody.
    46	
    47	---
    48	
    49	Two days up the Thornwater road to the fan, with the cart; the stretch walked at noon, quiet. They did not go up to Thornwater's gate. The slide had come down a mile short of the steading, and the road went over the toe of what it had left and the traverse went up its side, and there was a cairn, knocked half over, where the two parted. Seln set the stones back on it while the rest of them looked up.
    50	
    51	The fan was the worst ground of the route, and Cael catalogued it from the bottom: a spill of broken shale the width of a field, lying at the angle at which shale stops, every plate the size of a hand or a door and none of them fixed to anything. Somewhere on the mountain above it a shelf ran along over the gorge, and the shelf was the traverse, and the fan was where a piece of the shelf had come down.
    52	
    53	They took the cart up it on a rope, three ahead on the rope and Brom in the shafts holding the bed level, and it took the whole morning, and the calf said nine the entire way.
    54	
    55	---
    56	
    57	The bench, when they reached it, was a shelf.
    58	
    59	He wrote that down first, at the first halt, because it was the fact everything else stood on. A shelf of grey shale along the mountain's face, a cart and a half wide where they came onto it and sloping toward the outer edge as a roof slopes — not steep, but not flat, so that anything set down on it wanted to go one way. On the left the mountain went up in the same stone, plated, and two hundred meters up, by the eye, a second shelf ran along it, greener than theirs: an upper bench. On the right the shelf stopped, and past where it stopped there was nothing for a long way down, and at the bottom of the nothing a white line that was a river.
    60	
    61	The gorge had a sound. On the bench it resolved into water, far down, and wind coming up, and a third thing that was neither: the gorge took every sound they made and gave it back late, from the far wall. Brom set the cart's tail-board down at the first halt and the bang came back a breath afterward from across the gap, smaller, as if a second crew were working over there and were more careful. It was the loudest thing about the place, that it repeated you.
    62	
    63	Lira went ahead.
    64	
    65	She had taped the ankle at the fan's foot and again at the top, tighter, and then she went out along the bench at a run — not a burst; a burst was a shout, she had learned that on a gravel bar with seven dogs turning toward her — a hundred paces out over the shale with nothing under it but her, and came back at a walk, reading the ground with her feet. Shale that held a walker held nothing, she said; shale that held a runner held a wheel. She ran the bench in hundreds all afternoon and the cart followed in her prints, and at the evening halt she sat down before anyone and took the boot off and did not look at the ankle, and then looked at it.
    66	
    67	Karis mapped.
    68	
    69	She had paced a length of the void road against the folding rule on the first morning out of Lowmarch and had a number for her stride she trusted; now she walked the bench counting, the pen in the scarred hand, and at every place where the shelf narrowed she ran the cord across it edge to edge and wrote the width, and the count to it from the last width, and the pitch, which she took with the cord and a stone hung on it. Where the gorge came close she dropped a stone over and counted until the sound came up. What went into the book was a chain of numbers with a drawing beside it that looked like nothing at all — a line, kinked, with figures along it — and would look, to a man at Oxhollow with a cart, like the only true thing on the mountain.
    70	
    71	Brom kept the cart on the mountain.
    72	
    73	He said, at the second halt, that a cart on scree did not roll; it floated. The wheels sat on plates that sat on plates and the whole of it went where the slope wanted, which was over the edge, a finger's width at every turn of the wheel, unless the weight was over the axle and up-slope. He re-roped the boxes at every halt to keep the front one where it was, took the down-slope shaft himself as the counter, and walked the whole day with the cart's want against his chest, and it was — Cael watched for the word — a lock somebody else had built, being held shut by hand.
    74	
    75	Seln was ahead of Lira.
    76	
    77	Nobody saw him go past her; he was simply further along, and then further, and in the last hour of the light, when the cart came round a shoulder of the mountain, he was standing in a place where the bench widened to three carts and the rock above it leaned out over a floor that was flat and dry and out of the wind. He was standing in it as he was always standing in the place where they would stop, before anybody had said the word.
    78	
    79	"Here," he said, which was the whole report.
    80	
    81	Cael wrote.
    82	
    83	At every halt, on the cart's tail-board or his knee, as he had written in a notebook at the edge of every bout he had ever watched: the width at the narrow, in Karis's number and then in his own paces to check it; the count of narrowings from the fan — one, two, three by the first camp; where Seln had stood. Nothing on the mountain asked him what he was. It was the exact shape of a thing he had done for nothing for four years, except that at the far end of it there was a ford and seventy marks and a man who would read it.
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	*Seventy-third day. The bench, first camp. Three narrowings from the fan, none under a cart's width.*
    88	
    89	*Inventory. Nine confirmed, one anomaly, patience. Eighteen. Deployment since the Line: one, the ford, paid. Nothing drifting. Anchor wanted the inn door; there is no door up here. Calf: nine uphill, and the whole fan was uphill, and it's a leg again on the flat. Sixty-two marks earned. Forty-six in hand after the cart.*
    90	
    91	*Lira runs it before we walk it. Karis has a number for everything and a drawing that looks like nothing. Brom says the cart floats. I've written eleven widths since noon and none of them mine, and it's the first day of my life I've been paid by the line.*
    92	
    93	*The gorge says everything back late. That's the only loud thing on this mountain. Us, twice.*
    94	
    95	---
    96	
    97	He saw the first of them on the second day because it moved.
    98	
    99	They had come round the fourth narrowing — a cart's width and a hand, Karis's cord said, with the outer edge crumbling — and the bench opened again beyond it, and on the upper shelf two hundred meters above, where the green was, a piece of the mountain lifted its head.
   100	
   101	Lira, ahead, had stopped with a hand up; the cart stopped behind him with Brom's breath going out of it.
   102	
   103	The colour of the shale, which was why he had not seen it. Then, because he knew what he was looking for, the next one, and the next, and he counted them as he counted a crowd, left to right and again, and got twenty-nine and then thirty-one and then thirty, and kept thirty. Thirty animals along the upper bench, every one of them with its face down against the rock. Plated across the back in slabs that overlapped like the stone of a roof, grey going to rust as the shale went; the head small and low for the body and the legs short and thick under it; each one, by the eye at that distance, the size of the cart with its load and Brom in the shafts. They were eating lichen — the green was lichen, on the upper shelf where the wind did not scour it — and the sound he had taken for the gorge since the narrowing was them: a grinding, patient and low, thirty sets of teeth on stone, coming down to the lower bench and then back from the far wall, late.
   104	
