editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:123:The second item was the side she kept. On every slant she was below the mule. When the track turned, and the fall of the hill went over to the other hand, she passed behind the animal's tail without breaking step and came up on the low side again; and she did it at every turn of that hillside without once seeming to consider which side was the low one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:285:She let the hand fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:Thirty yards off, Brom let the tail-board fall. It banged, and nobody was there to stop it. He dragged out the left-hand box and got it onto his shoulder, and the middle one after it onto the other. Then he turned with a hundredweight on either side of his head and came back over the cracking shale at a trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:187:He did not hear her land. He heard the slide going over after her, tons of it, in a long hissing fall; and after that, at intervals, three or four heavier sounds, dull and separate, that were not stone and not a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:121:"It went for me, and I crossed it twice. Some of the stones on it are bedded and some are waiting to leave, and I could not tell the one from the other by looking; so I tried each with my weight before I trusted it, and I scratched a mark on every one that bore." He took out the narrow dull knife and showed its point, which was bright. "There are forty-four. A careful person may go over on those, facing the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:169:Cael had. Nobody could have missed it. Two-thirds of the way along the bay a blade of pale stone stood up out of the shale, quite alone, halfway between the foot of the face and the edge. It lay square across the fall of the ground, like a wall across a field, and it was as long as a barn and no thicker than a garden wall. At either end it was no higher than a man's waist, and in the middle it was twice his height, and the evening sun lay along its upper side and left the lower black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:51:"Hear me first. The country's rule has three things in it, and Pike's *loud* has the same three, since it's the same rule. For the hounds we found out which one mattered, and it cost four of us a month. I don't know which matters to those." She tipped her head at the roof, and at what was above it. "I shall have to find out, because the last column of his table is mine, and a carter who comes over this bench with a Force at his tail-board ought to be told what he may do with him. Of the three, there is one that I can test this evening and then take back."
