 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 10 — The Ford
     2	
     3	They were on the gravel with an hour of light to spare, and Seln had been in the river for some time already.
     4	
     5	It had all been arranged by noon. The steading's woman had stood in her yard with her sleeves rolled and told them what she would give and what she would keep, as she might have laid out the terms for a load of barley.
     6	
     7	"He's my eyes," she had said, of the bowman. "He'll stand on the bank where he can see the whole of it, and he'll not come down to you, whatever he sees. That's no unkindness. I've one bowman."
     8	
     9	"I wouldn't ask it of him," Cael had said.
    10	
    11	"No. You'd not know to." She had looked across at the carter, who was backing his mule between the shafts of an empty cart. "He'll be at the last bend above the beach, with her nose in a bag. Either way there'll be something to carry."
    12	
    13	"That's cheerful," Lira had said.
    14	
    15	"It's true," said Brom.
    16	
    17	Seln had come down the ladder last, and the strap across his chest was gone. The case lay in the loft between the second box and the third. He had looked back up at it twice from the yard, like a man who has left a child with strangers, and had said nothing about it to anybody.
    18	
    19	Now he was under the gravel bar's near flank, twenty yards down from its head, and Cael could not see him.
    20	
    21	He had watched him go in. It had been off the near beach, a little below the white stones, where the bottom fell away a stride from the edge. Seln had left his coat and boots under the cut bank and walked into the pool in his shirt with a knife that Cael saw then for the first time, a narrow dull thing no longer than a hand, which gave back no light. The water had taken him to the waist, and the chest. He had gone on until it closed over his shoulders, and only then, with nothing above the river but his face, had he done what he had not done since a fire at a crossroads.
    22	
    23	Nothing changed that Cael could have pointed to. A man's head was there on the green water, and then it was a thing that the eye did not care to rest on, like one stone among a great many. Behind eight others the ninth of his own fragments turned over, as a dog lifts its head at a voice it knows. It was Shadow, which had come to him out of that same Path, and he put it down.
    24	
    25	If Karis's second clause held, the river was hiding the Path while the Path hid the man. Seln had drifted in under the overhang, where the pool had cut the gravel back, and that was the last anybody saw of him.
    26	
    27	The rest of them were where the charcoal had put them. Brom stood on his stone in the near channel, five yards out, with his left heel against the lip and the bill in his right hand, facing the beach they had come down. Karis was at the bar's blunt head, beside a boulder the size of a loaf, and she had her small knife out; the blade of it shook a little, and her face did not. Cael and Lira had the far landing, where the two green grooves came up out of the water onto the gravel, with one of them on either side.
    28	
    29	On the cut bank above the near beach a lean man stood in the grass with an arrow on his string and its point toward his boots.
    30	
    31	Then there was nothing to do but stand.
    32	
    33	He had thought that there would be frogs. There were none; the only sound at that ford was the water going over the lip into the two pools, a long steady hiss like rain on a roof, and it covered everything. Nothing here would stop to warn him. He watched the far beach, and the grass above it, where three paths came down that he could not see, and the sun went lower behind them.
    34	
    35	It took the colour first. The river had been green all afternoon, and brown in the shallows, and gold where the light struck along it; and when the western ridge took the sun, the whole valley went grey in one breath, as it always did. He looked at the water sliding over the rock at his feet, and he knew what the man on the settle had meant.
   235	Seln was reading the board.
   236	
   237	Cael had never seen him do it. On their first day at Lowmarch he had simply been discovered at the plank's far end, looking at the last slip as though he had been there for some time. Now he started at the left-hand post, in the open, with his hands behind him, and went along the oak a slip at a time, as Cael himself went along it, giving each of the fourteen the same attention; and at the tenth he stopped.
   238	
   239	It was a piece of bark, and the sun had curled it. Beside the words somebody had drawn a ring with a little bucket hanging in it, and the drawing had faded nearly to nothing.
   240	
   241	*The well on the south road; four.*
   242	
   243	"That's been up since the day we got here," said Lira. She had come along on her stick to see. "I read it on the ninth. It's been there fifty days."
   244	
   245	"Fifty-one."
   246	
   247	"Why's nobody taken it?"
   248	
   249	"I do not know. I should think that there is a reason, and that it is not a pleasant one." Seln reached up and drew the tack, and it was the first slip that Cael had ever seen him touch. "It is half a day off, on level ground. The work at a well is done sitting, for the most part, or else hanging; and we are a crew with three good legs among five. I am the one of us who can hang."
   250	
