   273	## AFTER MOVEMENT 2 — chapters 8–14, days 51–69 past the Line (drafted 2026-10-11, author claude-opus-5-5, NO-SOURCE method)
   274	
   275	### Coordinator rulings (binding)
   276	- **The finding is earned and entered.** Stillhounds answer Path discharge; Karis's hypothesis (with its water clause) became a finding only after the ford, entered in the log in Cael's hand at her dictation. It leaves two things unaccounted: the hundred and ten yards, and dusk. Nothing more is known.
   277	- **Deployment since the Line: ONE** — a single Wind burst at the ford on day 53. None before; none after. Shadow is unused by Cael and stays sealed. Seln's use of his OWN native Shadow Path under water at the ford is not Cael's deployment and stands (it tested the water clause).
   278	- **The ford** is the upper of the two unnamed day-43 crossings, two to three hours below Thornwater's gate; walked day 52, fought day 53. Seven hounds; the bowman is the witness; the carter carts four of them home through the stretch in the dark.
   279	- **"They stop inside, where the tiers are": "they" are HEALERS.** The protected unit's full three-speech form is on the page at the steading's gate (ch13). Lira's thought about ranks is her own inference on the road. No healer appears; "the nearest walks a route and she's not due" stands; **Lira's ankle is not mended in this book** and her run on day 65 is a chosen risk.
   280	- **Quiet ground** remains three sentences from the carter's wife, spoken as one speech. Nobody asks. The bend on the carter's map two days east of Thornwater, round bare hide, is the only geography; Movement 4 inherits it.
   281	- **Teague is absent:** back on the 57th and out again, known only by Pike's report. No meeting.
   282	- **Bodies:** Cael bitten through the calf ("the calf in elevens"); Karis's hand cannot hold a pencil; Lira's ankle splinted, then walking; Brom's shoulder in a sling. Exact states are in the author's state section below, as corrected after repair.
   283	- **All new canon KEEP** (plan audit §6): the lip stones; "the third" as a man of the hold; the flint fire on the road lit on the strength of the finding, with the hold unchanged; the well's bad air; Pike pricing Brom's part at "a fifth of forty"; the closing bare tack.
   284	- **Length:** about 36,900 words against 36,000, accepted; the fight's 6,900 words accepted as earned. **Names:** none minted.
   285	
   286	### Review record
   287	All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial (pass; one register priority), cold read (three clarity items), source-distance (6 tracked passages, no dense scene), plan / canon / protected-text audit (RATIFY WITH LINE FIXES — twelve applied by the coordinator). Same-author repair r1 in progress when this block was written; the CLOSED note follows below when the recheck returns.
   288	
   289	### New canon minted (author's list)
   290	- **Where Thornwater's ford lies (flag):** the upper of the two shallow crossings of the valley's water that the party made on day 43; below the mouth of the stretch; between two and three hours' walk below the gate. The pack that paced the cart on the stretch is the ford's pack.
   291	- **The ford's ground (flag):** a river some fifty yards wide over a single ledge of grey rock, **the sill**, ten feet broad, with two wheel-grooves and green slime; shin-deep fast water over it; a slow glide above; below, a **lip**, and pools that fall away over a man's head. A gravel **bar** in mid-river, thirty yards by eight, lying with the stream, its blunt head at the sill and its tail running down between the two pools to a point. A near (Thornwater-side) and a far channel, twenty yards each; a near and a far beach; a cut bank a man's height above the near beach.
   292	- **The lip stones (flag):** a row of white stones, a yard apart, which the hold sets along the sill's downstream lip each year "when the water drops", so that a wheel can see where the rock stops. Five yards of the row were unset on day 52; the row is whole by day 58.
   293	- **The third (flag):** two men of the hold went down at noon on the 49th to set the last stones. One, a Force, lifted with his Path under water to free the other's leg and was let alone; he stood up on the dry bar at dusk and was taken. The other got his hand between a hound's jaws and his own throat, was left when the Force shouted, and walked home in the dark. Neither is named.
   294	- **What the hold knows:** "Dusk. Never noon."; the bowman has had the gate-walk thirty years, has seen one come up the grass four evenings in five, and **set the white stone himself in his first year, where they stop**. He cannot say why there. The 110 yards stay unaccounted for.
   295	- **The finding (flag), as dictated by Karis and written in Cael's log:** the stillhound hunts Path discharge; it hears and sees a little, and uses both only to keep its place and to try a quiet body with its teeth; water masks it at both ends (a wading hound goes to where the declaration was when it left dry ground; a Path held under water for an hour and a quarter drew nothing at eleven feet); there is no leader, only whichever moves first; they learn. Not accounted for: 110 yards; dusk.
   296	- **"Discharge"** is Karis's lecturer's word for what leaves a person when a Path is used; assessors call it presence and mark it; a station reads it off a plate.
   297	- **A fragment answering is felt by Cael as something going out**, like the others' discharge. Whether a hound can tell it from a Path is untested; he holds on the supposition that it cannot. The one burst drew three.
   298	- **Seven hounds at the ford:** six that lie up in the far bank's grass and come down by three paths; a seventh, older, white-muzzled, with a torn left ear, that lies on the bar's tail. They drink one at a time and will not wet a foot.
   299	- **Seln's Path under water (flag):** on day 53 he lifts his own Shadow for the first time since the crossroads fire, to the chin in the near pool, and lets it go before he rises. His knife: narrow, dull, a hand long. "The cut" is his trade's word for the man placed where the subject will run.
   300	- **The hold's arrangements:** the bowman goes down as the hirer's witness and stands on the cut bank; the carter waits with the cart at the last bend; Seln's case stays in the loft between the second box and the third for that one evening.
   301	- **Injuries, as fixed on the page:** Brom, left forearm, two furrows from wrist to mid-arm, to the bone, fourteen stitches; opened again at the wrist on day 59 (a hand put out to steady the cart in a wet rut), seen at the fire that night; stitches out on day 64; closed clean and re-wrapped on day 66. Lira, right ankle, the ligaments, bound flat to a barrel-stave; a stick from the bowman on day 56, left against the inn wall on day 64. Karis, two cuts across each palm; bound fists, then strips; a pencil on day 68. Cael, left calf, in at the outside and out at the inside; eleven strides, then twenty (day 62), then "a leg on level ground" and nine uphill (day 69).
   302	- **Thornwater:** a long house with a hearth across one end and a trestle; fourteen people at supper; a sack-mending trestle in the barn; the hold charges one mark for linen and thread. East carts fetch grain on day 58 by the east gate.
   303	- **The carter's map (flag):** east of Thornwater, two days of ticks out, the double line leaves its straight, goes round a patch of bare hide and comes back to its line, with nothing drawn inside the bend. Karis calls it "a quiet place on the hide"; that word draws the carter's wife's three sentences.
   304	- **A flint fire is not a declaration (flag):** on the 59th, above the wash, the crew lights its first road fire since the fifth day, on the finding's first sentence. The hold itself stands unchanged. The Thornwater carter sits at it and says that he will not do it alone.
   305	- **Lowmarch:** undirected eastbound mail stops at Pike's plank; he gives out each piece twice and reads letters at dusk to people who have no letters; a spike under the plank holds finished slips; he rewrites the whole wall on fresh sized sailcloth when the order changes; a crew entered as *the shingle pair* is thirteenth from day 60.
   306	- **Teague:** back down the cold road on the 57th; read the struck slip twice and the five's line; took two holds' slips from the north end of the plank and went up the north water at next light. Not seen.
   307	- **The well (flag):** at the south road's fork, half a day out on level ground; a dry-stone curb, a windlass; paid from the standing-money. It went sour at the thaw; a carter's boy was drawn up asleep. Foul air lay fifteen feet deep on the water at thirty feet. Pike's line: *The well at the south fork. Air cleared, shaft drawn, six stones set. Four marks.*
   308	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the hold's boy on the leaning mule; the man with the bound arm; the clouded-eyed east carter, his wife, and the small boy on her foot-board; the shingle pair; the southbound carter whose son went down the well.
   309	
   310	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   311	**Calendar.** The evening of the **sixty-ninth day past the Line**, under the lower inn's eave at Lowmarch. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the thaw" in three places; "frost" once). Days on the page: 51, 52, 53, 54, 55–57 (the stick on the 56th; the carter back on the 57th), 58, 59, 60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 65, 66, 68, 69. Told, not shown: the dusk of the 49th at the ford; the 50th. Weather: dry roads; mist on the river at first light; cold coming up out of the wash after dark. The next movement opens on the morning of day 70.
   312	
   313	**Where everyone is.** All five at Lowmarch, sleeping in the meadow, with the three boxes in the loft over the ferryman's drum. Teague and his three are out on two holds' slips up the north water.
   314	
   315	**Cael.** Eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted anomaly.** **Deployment since the Line: one** (a single Wind burst at the ford on day 53, in front of the hold's bowman). Shadow unused; it lifted at dusk through the wait at the ford and again on the walk down the bar and was refused. Ember offered a point in the hound's mouth over Karis and was refused. The Iron read stays shut. Left calf bitten through on day 53: a leg on level ground, nine strides uphill. He has not asked Seln for his report, has not answered Lira's question, has not answered Hesk's letter, and has stopped counting Lira's runs. He knows that he had a grandmother who crossed the Line once on a trade run; no name, no line of descent.
   316	
   317	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. Right ankle: ligaments, never seen by a healer, bound eleven days; no stick since day 64; runs on the gravel bar from day 65; "it bends less, and later". No burst at the ford or since. She killed one hound with the long knife. Her question stands unanswered in Cael's log.
   318	
   319	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. He declared once at the ford, on purpose, for about three breaths, and dropped it with the hound's teeth shut on his arm; he drowned that hound with his shoulder and the current. Left forearm: closed clean on day 66, re-wrapped by his own choice, "shut, not sound". He has carried nothing since day 53. He keeps Pike's six words ("Same as anyone. What'd he do?") on a dead slip inside his shirt.
   320	
   321	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. One point lit and put out at the ford; none since. Two pink lines across each palm; a pencil again on day 68. Holds: the hypothesis leaf; the finding (in Cael's log, by her dictation, and in the field archive in Lira's hand); a leaf headed *This side.*; the carter's map; and, in the middle box, the carter's wife's three sentences with *everybody knows* underlined twice. She has asked nobody what quiet ground is, Seln included.
   322	
   323	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze (not said in public). Lifted his Path once, under water, on day 53; killed the seventh hound. Unmarked. Walked the south road to the well first, alone, and said nothing. Has said aloud that he keeps waiting to be asked for his report. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut**; it spent the evening of day 53 in Thornwater's loft.
   324	
   325	**Objects and money.** Three boxes over the ferry drum. The reserve unopened. Board purse: **sixty-two marks earned** (18 + ford 40 + well 4); **fifty-two in hand** (one mark on flour and bacon on day 51; one to the hold for linen and thread; coppers unitemised). Hesk's satchel holds the log (with the finding and the seal entry), the old volume, the observation notebook, Pike's two lines on bark, and Hesk's letter. Cael's coat has lost its left sleeve. The ash stick is propping an old carter's shaft under the ash.
   326	
   327	**Pike's book and the wall.** **Six lines** under the five names: the protected ford line (said on day 60) and *The well at the south fork. Air cleared, shaft drawn, six stones set. Four marks.* (day 64). **Twelfth of thirteen; Teague eleven up.** The ford slip is with the finished slips, struck with one line through Teague's name and five strokes beneath, and Thornwater's mark at the foot. Fourteen slips on the plank on day 60; a bare tack set by the left-hand post on the evening of day 69 for a hold's slip expected from the north-west.
   328	
   329	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: the five, and nobody else. The hold's bowman saw one Wind burst and took it for a Path. The party now holds the stillhound finding in five sentences. **Quiet ground is three sentences from a carter's wife**; nobody has asked Pike or anyone. Thornwater knows the five as the crew that came up from its ford. Teague has read the struck slip and the line and said nothing; no meeting.
   330	
   331	**Open threads.** Two hounds gone down the river, logged as open. Why a hundred and ten yards; why dusk. What quiet ground is, and what a road bends round two days east of Thornwater. Lira's question. Hesk's letter and the roof. What Teague will say when they have cost him something. A hold's slip, with a ford in it, due on the oak in the morning.
   332	
