editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:323:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:1:# Chapter 18 — The Eighth
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:119:He had it ready, and it was not the one that she expected. On any floor he had ever stood on he would have decided this himself, and in a breath, since the plan was always his. But the thing in front of him was a weight and a gap, and whether a back could take the one over the other; and there was a person on that shelf who had known such sums since he was twelve years old, and it was not Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:The table stood under a lean-to roof against the gate-house wall, and nobody in Oxhollow had put so much as a cup on it for twenty-five days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:129:"Thornwater's on my route in nine days. You'll be on the board by then. Come find me. I want to read you when you're not broken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:It was not her hands, then. She had half thought, on the hill out of Oxhollow, that something in them might have begun to fail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:Three ribs, broken on the seventy-eighth. Twenty-one days to knit, in anybody; less, in him, by what she had felt. She would be at Thornwater on the ninety-third, and he would be fifteen days from the break. Not whole. She could not run the long one over a break. She would have her two days at that gate, and the carter's hand, which she owed, and whatever was on the bench; and then the round would take her on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:215:The hand troubled her more than she liked. A split across the palm is a small thing on the day, and she had left it clean and wrapped, and told the man to keep it out of a rein. He would not have kept it out of a rein. He had a load for Thornwater and one pair of hands, and he had given her the news of a late crew when he might have held his tongue and had his mending. By the ninety-third that hand would be fifteen days old. She owed it to him as she owed nothing else on the round, and she meant to pay it before she sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:253:The road came round the shoulder of the hill, and Oxhollow lay under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:305:"You cleared Thornwater's ford with five and lost a cart on the high bench with five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:315:Cael went through it for the place where he could say so. He thought of a smith in Lowmarch, and six marks owed, and a tail-board that had not banged. He thought of what a ford-keeper had counted out, and what the ford-keeper had said he was paying for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:327:*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow, at the ford. The map is delivered: sixty-three lines, and the last one says no. Seventy marks, counted. Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and sixteen. Six owed to the smith for a cart that is in a gorge.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:It was Wind, and it was hers, and it was the whole of it. He had not heard that sound from her since a painted floor at Norhold, and she had never, there or anywhere, let it go so hard. She was not on the falling stone. She was twenty feet east of it, in the air, and then she was standing on sound ledge with both arms flung wide. Behind her a hundred feet of shelf was going down into the blue with a roar that grew as it fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:She had no breath to spare for it. She had both hands lifted a little in front of her, the palms with their pink lines turned toward the hill, and she had begun to do the thing that Ternhall had taught her before she was fifteen. *Points first, always. Joins last.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:257:She had been told at Fenmark that a burst was a choice, and at Norhold she had made a rank of choosing well. For sixty days on gravel bars and roads she had refused it a stride at a time, on purpose, and she had been proud of how good she had got. Not one of those refusals was anywhere near her now. The ground went, and her body did what a body with Wind in it does when the ground goes, and it did not send up to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:235:"That's a carter at the foot of a hill who had a split hand, and told me while I was looking at it that a crew was late off the top. I said I'd do his hand that evening, and then I went up to you." There was no reproach in it. "He's got it in a rag still. He hauls to Thornwater, and I'll find him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:301:Oxhollow lay in the bend of a broad shallow river, and they came down to its ford in the middle of the eighty-fourth morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:313:"You'll be the Lowmarch crew that took my paper. A carter said the one-armed man had given it to five." He looked past them, at the empty road, and at the river. "Where's your cart?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:11:"Because every line under it means *this* cart," she said. "A farm wagon from Oxhollow is a foot wider, for all I know. I can tell him what the shelf is. I can't tell him what he'll put on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:187:"And then down the other side to a ford, and a man counts out seventy." She had her arms folded. Her right foot was set a little forward, on its whole sole, and she was looking along the ledge as he had seen her look along a painted floor at Norhold before the bell. "It's the best ground on the whole mountain. It's rock from end to end. There isn't a plate on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:51:"You don't know that, and I don't know it either. I've never spent it all before; nobody at Ternhall would have let me." Her voice had no fright in it, and that was worse than fright. "Nine points and eight channels, held for eight seconds across sixty yards. I should be able to give you the cost of that to a decimal. I can't find the figure. I can't find where I keep figures."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:357:"I can't mend what's in you, since I can't find where it's standing," she said. "So you get what a carter gets. Cloth, and tape in the morning." She knotted the end. "A man at the foot of this hill told me the mountain had come down two days back, and that a crew off the Lowmarch plank had gone up it with a cart and not come off the other side. He said you were late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:"The second he had from a carter, who had it from his father. Men used to come up a side water off the Thornwater road with carts, for roofing stone. It lies ready split at the foot of a great slide, and need only be loaded. One year they did not come back down, and the next year nobody went up to learn why." He lifted his eyes to the wheel-ruts under their feet, with the turf grown over them. "I had not supposed, until this morning, that I should be standing in the second tale."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:"I asked him that in those words. He said, the kind a man would not be on a road." Seln spread his hands a little. "He means all three, and he does not know which. It is the rule that Thornwater keeps, for the reason that Thornwater gave us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:205:"Seventy lines." Lira began to laugh, low, mindful of the rule. "Seventy marks. He's being paid by the line. A mark a line." She pointed at the book in his hand. "You've written in one of those every night since a lane in Ardenmere, and I've watched you do it, and nobody ever gave you a copper. Now there's a man with a ford who'll give you a mark every time you rule one off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:277:"Oxhollow's side," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:21:*The high traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. To be walked with a cart, and mapped. Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:31:"It's the same thing with a wheel on it." Pike laid his one hand on the paper. "Oxhollow's six days north-west of this plank, and Thornwater's two days east of it. A cart from the one that wants grain from the other comes round by my ferry, and it's eight days, and eight home. There's high ground between the two holds. The salt crews say there's a shelf along the top of it, a bench, with a gorge down one side. A mule string went over it once, before I'd this book, and came down in three days." He looked at the horned head, twice. "Somebody's told the ford so. The ford wants to know whether it's true before it sends a wheel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:35:"Oxhollow's a ford with a fence round it. The keeper takes a copper a wheel, the same as my ferryman, and it's the keeper's paper. He'd sooner his wheels came back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read, as he had once sorted a board in Ardenmere by what each card would cost him. There was a fence for somebody on the north water, at five marks. A string of mules wanted walking to the turf fire, westward, and that one he passed over without stopping. A carter on the south road wanted a wheel fetched. There was hay to be got in at the upper inn, for a mark and the hay-loft to sleep in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:147:"Thornwater has a loft," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:149:"Thornwater's loft is full of the east carts' grain, and it's half a day past the place where we turn off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:161:The cart went up to the saddle between two people. It had no beast, for there was not a mule in Lowmarch to be hired for a mountain by a crew that might not bring it back. Seln took the near shaft and Cael the off, and they leaned, and the narrow wheels went round. On the flat of the meadow it was nothing. On the hill Cael counted nine strides and felt the calf begin to close, and at the tenth Lira's shoulder came in beside his without a word, and took the shaft, and he fell back to the tail and walked with his hand on a box until the leg forgave him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:179:"I've lifted nothing. I'm leaning." His whole plain face had lightened, for the first time since a river. "I leaned on a mule for five days at Thornwater and nobody stopped me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:201:*Seventy-first day. The east road, above the wash. Taken at the plank yesterday: a hold's slip, Oxhollow's, for the high traverse between that hold and Thornwater. Walked with a cart and mapped. Seventy marks, paid at Oxhollow on the map. We walk it from this end and come down on his.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:219:They left the Thornwater road in the middle of the next morning, at the lower of its two crossings, and none of them had ever looked to the left there before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:239:"It's late," said Lira, delighted. "Listen to how late it is. I've had an echo before, in the long hall at Fenmark, and it came straight back at you like a slap. This one goes away and thinks about it."

