editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:357:By evening the stick had gone from the wall. He saw it next day under the ash, where an old carter had wedged it beneath his cart's shaft for a prop, and he did not think that the bowman would have minded.
169:It was a halting-ground like the others, a level of beaten earth with a ring of chock-stones, and in the middle of it stood a curb of dry stone as high as his waist. Two posts carried a windlass over it, with an iron crank at one end and no rope on the drum, and the trough beside it had gone green and then dried.
211:Brom showed them what to cut, standing over Seln with the bill and pointing with his chin at this branch of thorn and that. They bound up a faggot as wide as the shaft, and made it fast to the ferryman's rope and the rope fast to the drum. Lira sat on an upturned bucket with both hands on the crank, and wound the great prickly bundle down into the dark and up again, and down, like somebody churning butter in a giant's kitchen, while Cael stood on one leg and took the other side of the handle.
225:He went down it the next morning, on the rope, and it was the first argument Seln had lost since a shelf of turf.
229:"You're the only one of us who can stand at that crank and brace. Lira can wind, sitting. If I come up limp, somebody with two legs will have to get me over the curb." Cael was tying a loop to sit in. "And a leg's no use to anybody on a rope."
255:The frost had heaved the top course of the lining at the thaw, and six stones had gone in, and he felt for them with his feet. He stooped for each one, to the shoulder in that water, and got it into the loop, and jerked the rope twice, and it went up past him, dripping, toward the coin of light. He brought up the bucket, and a length of chain; and he brought up something else, which had gone in after water a long while ago and was past being anything, and he sent that up with his face turned to the wall.
279:Nobody called down to ask him why. The rope came tight under him at once, and the stone went out of his arms into the water.
281:The coin grew. He watched it grow, and observed with some interest that his hands on the rope had turned stupid, and that he was glad of the loop; and then there were fingers under his arms, a great many of them, and the edge of the curb, and grass against his cheek.
317:She made no answer to that, but bent and picked up the faggot's rope.

